I guess at this point in time,

57
00:02:47,550 --> 00:02:49,940
we all know these examples.

58
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:54,290
Because I think these days
we are becoming more and more

59
00:02:54,290 --> 00:02:58,680
interested in native multi-modal
generation, not just images.

60
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:01,700
But nonetheless, I just
wanted to start off

61
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:03,620
by giving you a
couple of examples

62
00:03:03,620 --> 00:03:05,520
from the text-to-image arena.

63
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:07,470
My favorite is the last one--

64
00:03:07,470 --> 00:03:10,380
tiny astronaut hatching
from an egg on the moon.

65
00:03:10,380 --> 00:03:14,910
I think human imagination
really took off there.

66
00:03:14,910 --> 00:03:15,630
But yeah.

67
00:03:15,630 --> 00:03:18,990
You can see apart from DALL-E
3 all these are open models,

68
00:03:18,990 --> 00:03:21,740
and the photorealism
aspects of these models

69
00:03:21,740 --> 00:03:23,670
are quite impressive.

70
00:03:23,670 --> 00:03:26,120
So yeah.

71
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:31,100
And then I want to also
give you an infographic

72
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:35,242
of how I like to think about
diffusion models in general.

73
00:03:35,242 --> 00:03:36,950
So I like to think
about diffusion models

74
00:03:36,950 --> 00:03:37,800
as the following.

75
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:40,340
So when you take a
random noise vector

76
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:44,180
and what happens as you denoise
it over a period of time,

77
00:03:44,180 --> 00:03:46,470
so that it becomes a
photorealistic image.

78
00:03:46,470 --> 00:03:49,520
And if you take a
look closely, you

79
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:53,680
will notice that it's an
iterative process unlike GANs.

80
00:03:53,680 --> 00:03:57,030
GANs are one-shot in nature, but
diffusion models are iterative.

81
00:03:57,030 --> 00:03:58,510
It's sequential in nature.

82
00:03:58,510 --> 00:04:02,490
So in a way, we are essentially
denoising the random noise

83
00:04:02,490 --> 00:04:07,050
that we had started off
with, until and unless we

84
00:04:07,050 --> 00:04:09,700
are done with the kind of
image that we are looking for.

85
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:11,650
So just know that it's
iterative in nature.

86
00:04:11,650 --> 00:04:13,715
That's the main takeaway
from this slide.

87
00:04:13,715 --> 00:04:16,480

88
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:19,660
When you start conditioning the
denoising process with text,

89
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:21,850
for example, you can
condition in lots of ways.

90
00:04:21,850 --> 00:04:25,500
But let's say, I think text
is one of the more liberating

91
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:26,410
conditions.

92
00:04:26,410 --> 00:04:29,550
As you start conditioning the
denoising process with text,

93
00:04:29,550 --> 00:04:33,240
you get abstract
creatures like this.

94
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:37,170
But yeah, you will start
feeling very liberated

95
00:04:37,170 --> 00:04:41,040
with text-to-image
as I like to believe.

96
00:04:41,040 --> 00:04:45,780
And then let's take a step back
and start developing a mental

97
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:50,310
model of what it takes to
have a fairly state-of-the-art

98
00:04:50,310 --> 00:04:51,490
text-to-image model.

99
00:04:51,490 --> 00:04:54,030
Let's say I want to
get from this text

100
00:04:54,030 --> 00:04:56,550
prompt a cat looking like a
tiger, and from all the way

101
00:04:56,550 --> 00:04:59,530
up to the image on the top.

102
00:04:59,530 --> 00:05:00,730
What does it take?

103
00:05:00,730 --> 00:05:04,240
What are the components
that we are looking for?

104
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:05,530
How should they be connected?

105
00:05:05,530 --> 00:05:11,580
So I want to give you a
connected graph of how

106
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:13,440
the different components
should be connected

107
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:15,660
so that we can
backtrack them and start

108
00:05:15,660 --> 00:05:18,330
developing more intuition.

109
00:05:18,330 --> 00:05:20,170
So of course, when
you have text,

110
00:05:20,170 --> 00:05:22,830
you need to have some
of embedding so that you

111
00:05:22,830 --> 00:05:25,080
can work with those
embeddings, and we

112
00:05:25,080 --> 00:05:26,710
have text encoders for them.

113
00:05:26,710 --> 00:05:28,990
And state-of-the-art
diffusion models,

114
00:05:28,990 --> 00:05:31,840
they usually rely on more
than one text encoders.

115
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:33,460
For example, Stable
Diffusion three,

116
00:05:33,460 --> 00:05:36,100
it relies on three text
encoders-- not one, not two,

117
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:36,970
but three.

118
00:05:36,970 --> 00:05:40,230
So you have your text
prompt, you pass it off

119
00:05:40,230 --> 00:05:43,900
to text encoders, and you
then have the embeddings.

120
00:05:43,900 --> 00:05:46,120
And as we saw in
the earlier slide,

121
00:05:46,120 --> 00:05:48,450
we are starting off with
some random noise drawn

122
00:05:48,450 --> 00:05:49,990
from a Gaussian distribution.

123
00:05:49,990 --> 00:05:53,170
So we are starting with
some noisy latents,

124
00:05:53,170 --> 00:05:55,860
and then you have
your text embeddings.

125
00:05:55,860 --> 00:05:59,970
And then you have
some time step,

126
00:05:59,970 --> 00:06:02,650
which you draw-- which you
do the math with a scheduler.

127
00:06:02,650 --> 00:06:05,710
I'm going to come to the
scheduler component in a minute.

128
00:06:05,710 --> 00:06:07,930
But you have your conditions
at this point in time.

129
00:06:07,930 --> 00:06:10,620
You have your text embeddings,
you have your noisy latents,

130
00:06:10,620 --> 00:06:14,190
and you have some time step.

131
00:06:14,190 --> 00:06:18,900
And then you have your
core diffusion network,

132
00:06:18,900 --> 00:06:23,140
and you pass all these inputs
to your core diffusion network.

133
00:06:23,140 --> 00:06:25,360
And it's a sequential process.

134
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:28,540
You run the diffusion network
over a period of time,

135
00:06:28,540 --> 00:06:30,760
as we saw in the earlier slide.

136
00:06:30,760 --> 00:06:34,050
And then it's going to give
you some refined latent.

137
00:06:34,050 --> 00:06:36,450
And then you give it
to some decoder model

138
00:06:36,450 --> 00:06:38,770
and you have your image out.

139
00:06:38,770 --> 00:06:42,240
Now, I would like to call out
two broad classes of diffusion

140
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:42,940
models.

141
00:06:42,940 --> 00:06:45,660
This overview
resembles the pixels--

142
00:06:45,660 --> 00:06:47,233
the latent space
diffusion models.

143
00:06:47,233 --> 00:06:49,150
But there's another class
of diffusion models,

144
00:06:49,150 --> 00:06:51,150
which is called the pixel
space diffusion model.

145
00:06:51,150 --> 00:06:53,990
But the recent-- or the state
of the diffusion models, they

146
00:06:53,990 --> 00:06:56,550
are all latent space-based,
because pixel space

147
00:06:56,550 --> 00:06:59,340
is quite prohibitive and
computationally intensive

148
00:06:59,340 --> 00:07:00,330
in nature.

149
00:07:00,330 --> 00:07:04,070
That's why it's more common
to see latent space diffusion

150
00:07:04,070 --> 00:07:04,570
models.

151
00:07:04,570 --> 00:07:08,280
So that's why you see
noisy latents there

152
00:07:08,280 --> 00:07:09,760
and not raw pixels.

153
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:12,570
And if you were
to generalize this

154
00:07:12,570 --> 00:07:15,570
to other continuous
modalities, the basic idea

155
00:07:15,570 --> 00:07:20,430
here is how do you represent
the raw modality data points,

156
00:07:20,430 --> 00:07:23,090
and how do you compute
intermediate representations

157
00:07:23,090 --> 00:07:23,590
of them.

158
00:07:23,590 --> 00:07:25,900
So if you were to do
this on the audio space,

159
00:07:25,900 --> 00:07:28,840
you can think of some
similar analogies.

160
00:07:28,840 --> 00:07:32,940
But long story cut short,
for a fairly well-performing

161
00:07:32,940 --> 00:07:35,670
text-to-image system, these
are roughly the components

162
00:07:35,670 --> 00:07:36,610
that you need.

163
00:07:36,610 --> 00:07:39,270
So yeah.

164
00:07:39,270 --> 00:07:44,010
And then let's now start
developing some notations so

165
00:07:44,010 --> 00:07:46,810
that we can start discussing
about how these models are

166
00:07:46,810 --> 00:07:47,310
trained.

167
00:07:47,310 --> 00:07:49,680
And finally, how
you should perform

168
00:07:49,680 --> 00:07:51,250
inference with these models.

169
00:07:51,250 --> 00:07:55,870
So again, I'm giving this
examples with images.

170
00:07:55,870 --> 00:08:01,020
But these are fairly agnostic--
modality agnostic, apart

171
00:08:01,020 --> 00:08:01,660
from text.

172
00:08:01,660 --> 00:08:04,500
Of course, this won't
work on discrete tokens.

173
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:06,810
So let's say I have
some original image

174
00:08:06,810 --> 00:08:09,330
and I'm drawing some noise
from a standard Gaussian

175
00:08:09,330 --> 00:08:10,270
distribution.

176
00:08:10,270 --> 00:08:13,470
And then let's say I am
also drawing some time step

177
00:08:13,470 --> 00:08:15,370
from a uniform distribution.

178
00:08:15,370 --> 00:08:18,600
And then I have a particular
noise schedule and also

179
00:08:18,600 --> 00:08:21,310
some terms that are
controlling the noise schedule.

180
00:08:21,310 --> 00:08:23,760
And then let's say I have
some conditioning vector.

181
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:26,040
And by conditioning
vector I essentially mean

182
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:28,050
some text embeddings
or some other form

183
00:08:28,050 --> 00:08:32,159
of structural control,
like let's say, depth map,

184
00:08:32,159 --> 00:08:33,970
segmentation maps and so on.

185
00:08:33,970 --> 00:08:37,860
And then you have your diffusion
model that you will learn.

186
00:08:37,860 --> 00:08:40,470
And during training,
what we basically do

187
00:08:40,470 --> 00:08:45,730
is we compute some intermediate
representations of the images.

188
00:08:45,730 --> 00:08:49,000
Or let's say if you are
working on the pixel space,

189
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:53,850
you add small amount of
noise to your clean images

190
00:08:53,850 --> 00:08:55,440
and you make the
model predict what

191
00:08:55,440 --> 00:08:57,970
was the amount of
noise that was added.

192
00:08:57,970 --> 00:09:00,810
This is one very popular
and widely adopted

193
00:09:00,810 --> 00:09:03,190
parameterization of
diffusion network training.

194
00:09:03,190 --> 00:09:05,890
It's called the epsilon
objective, if you will.

195
00:09:05,890 --> 00:09:07,600
And that's basically it.

196
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:09,810
So we basically
make the model learn

197
00:09:09,810 --> 00:09:12,310
what was the amount of
noise that was added.

198
00:09:12,310 --> 00:09:13,960
So that's training.

199
00:09:13,960 --> 00:09:19,170
And during sampling, we repeat
the noise prediction part

200
00:09:19,170 --> 00:09:23,370
in a sequential manner, until
and unless we arrive at an image

201
00:09:23,370 --> 00:09:25,510
that we feel good about.

202
00:09:25,510 --> 00:09:30,780
So we start from a random
noise, and we denoise it

203
00:09:30,780 --> 00:09:33,150
over a period of time with
some condition, which can be

204
00:09:33,150 --> 00:09:34,810
text embeddings, for example.

205
00:09:34,810 --> 00:09:38,610
And then we repeat the denoising
process over a period of time,

206
00:09:38,610 --> 00:09:40,860
until and unless we
arrive at a data point

207
00:09:40,860 --> 00:09:42,870
that we feel good about.

208
00:09:42,870 --> 00:09:44,040
So yeah.

209
00:09:44,040 --> 00:09:47,640
And for flow-matching which is
becoming more and more common

210
00:09:47,640 --> 00:09:49,450
these days, for example, Flux.

211
00:09:49,450 --> 00:09:52,080
If you have heard of the model
Flux or Stable Diffusion 3,

212
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:54,180
these are all
flow-matching-based.

213
00:09:54,180 --> 00:09:59,260
In flow-matching, the
paths become more straight.

214
00:09:59,260 --> 00:10:02,370
We try to connect
noise and clean data

215
00:10:02,370 --> 00:10:03,760
through a straight path.

216
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:05,580
That's why you see the
linear interpolation

217
00:10:05,580 --> 00:10:07,120
equation in the first point.

218
00:10:07,120 --> 00:10:10,230
And we try to predict
the target velocity

219
00:10:10,230 --> 00:10:13,300
field with a neural network.

220
00:10:13,300 --> 00:10:18,240
So the key takeaway here is we
try to connect noise and clean

221
00:10:18,240 --> 00:10:20,220
data through a straight line.

222
00:10:20,220 --> 00:10:22,050
But in diffusion
models, the path

223
00:10:22,050 --> 00:10:24,070
is not assumed to
be a straight line.

224
00:10:24,070 --> 00:10:26,320
So that's why a lot
of simplification,

225
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:30,030
you will see in flow-matching.

226
00:10:30,030 --> 00:10:32,740
But long story cut
short, in this talk,

227
00:10:32,740 --> 00:10:36,040
we are more interested in the
network component of things.

228
00:10:36,040 --> 00:10:37,980
We are not interested
in how you should

229
00:10:37,980 --> 00:10:41,220
add the noise, what
particular noise schedules you

230
00:10:41,220 --> 00:10:43,180
should follow, and so on.

231
00:10:43,180 --> 00:10:46,560
We are more focused on
the parameterization

232
00:10:46,560 --> 00:10:49,170
side of things in this talk.

233
00:10:49,170 --> 00:10:52,140
So yeah.

234
00:10:52,140 --> 00:10:58,470
And let's now start discussing
the components we might need,

235
00:10:58,470 --> 00:11:03,820
the components that are
expected in a diffusion model.

236
00:11:03,820 --> 00:11:06,480
What are the core
requirements that we

237
00:11:06,480 --> 00:11:09,450
want to see in a
diffusion model to have

238
00:11:09,450 --> 00:11:15,000
so that it performs within
some bound of expectations.

239
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:19,710
So we have to figure a way out
to deal with the noisy inputs.

240
00:11:19,710 --> 00:11:23,070
As we saw, noise, we can't
really get rid of the noise

241
00:11:23,070 --> 00:11:26,760
because that gives
us the foundation.

242
00:11:26,760 --> 00:11:30,300
And usually in pixel
space your shapes

243
00:11:30,300 --> 00:11:34,170
will look something like
this if your channels first.

244
00:11:34,170 --> 00:11:38,830
And if you're working
on the latent space,

245
00:11:38,830 --> 00:11:41,380
it will, of course,
be compressed a bit.

246
00:11:41,380 --> 00:11:44,990
And we usually always deal
with the latent space diffusion

247
00:11:44,990 --> 00:11:45,490
model.

248
00:11:45,490 --> 00:11:48,540
So it helps to have
the shapes that you

249
00:11:48,540 --> 00:11:53,250
would expect to see in your
models that will flow through.

250
00:11:53,250 --> 00:11:55,380
And then you will have to
deal with the conditions

251
00:11:55,380 --> 00:11:58,680
because all the text-to-image
models that you see,

252
00:11:58,680 --> 00:12:00,280
text is your condition.

253
00:12:00,280 --> 00:12:02,910
So you'll have to deal
with your conditions.

254
00:12:02,910 --> 00:12:04,600
And of course time step.

255
00:12:04,600 --> 00:12:07,980
So the amount of noise that
gets added to your latents,

256
00:12:07,980 --> 00:12:09,640
it should depend
on the time step.

257
00:12:09,640 --> 00:12:13,650
Let's say if your time step is
10, the amount of noise that

258
00:12:13,650 --> 00:12:15,760
will get added to
your data points,

259
00:12:15,760 --> 00:12:18,910
it will be different
from the time step--

260
00:12:18,910 --> 00:12:23,590
if the time step were to be
somewhere like 1,000 or 100.

261
00:12:23,590 --> 00:12:27,180
So that's why time step is
also a very crucial condition

262
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:30,300
because it basically
tells the model that

263
00:12:30,300 --> 00:12:34,750
which point in the
trajectory it's in.

264
00:12:34,750 --> 00:12:37,030
Should it denoise
less, should it denoise

265
00:12:37,030 --> 00:12:39,730
more aggressively, and so on.

266
00:12:39,730 --> 00:12:41,440
And then you have
got other forms

267
00:12:41,440 --> 00:12:44,620
of conditions such as
class, maybe tiger, bird

268
00:12:44,620 --> 00:12:48,490
or just like natural language.

269
00:12:48,490 --> 00:12:52,520
And then we have to also model
the dependencies quite a bit.

270
00:12:52,520 --> 00:12:56,630
How should the noisy inputs
interact with your conditions.

271
00:12:56,630 --> 00:12:59,200
Of course, you might want
to think about these things

272
00:12:59,200 --> 00:13:01,370
through the lens of
cross-attention and so on,

273
00:13:01,370 --> 00:13:05,050
but we'll get to
that in a moment.

274
00:13:05,050 --> 00:13:08,810
And then how should the
final outputs be produced?

275
00:13:08,810 --> 00:13:11,380
Should we just flat
out decode everything

276
00:13:11,380 --> 00:13:14,510
if we were to deal with
transformer-based architectures?

277
00:13:14,510 --> 00:13:16,480
Should we be
upsampling if we were

278
00:13:16,480 --> 00:13:20,920
to deal with pure convolutional
architectures, and so on?

279
00:13:20,920 --> 00:13:23,630
And usually, for the
diffusion network,

280
00:13:23,630 --> 00:13:26,890
the input shape exactly
equals the output shape,

281
00:13:26,890 --> 00:13:30,040
until and unless you are dealing
with a separate parameterization

282
00:13:30,040 --> 00:13:31,490
of diffusion.

283
00:13:31,490 --> 00:13:33,110
There are other
parameterizations,

284
00:13:33,110 --> 00:13:36,400
but it's way more common
to see the inputs that

285
00:13:36,400 --> 00:13:38,260
are flowing through
the diffusion model

286
00:13:38,260 --> 00:13:41,050
will equal the outputs
that the diffusion

287
00:13:41,050 --> 00:13:44,650
model is supposed to produce.

288
00:13:44,650 --> 00:13:47,690
And then, here's a
bit of a history.

289
00:13:47,690 --> 00:13:51,440
So DDPM, all the early
work in this area--

290
00:13:51,440 --> 00:13:55,330
DDPM latent diffusion
models, they all

291
00:13:55,330 --> 00:13:58,280
used a UNet-based architectures.

292
00:13:58,280 --> 00:14:00,490
And I think
historically speaking,

293
00:14:00,490 --> 00:14:05,240
UNets have dominated this area
for quite a bit, like StyleGAN--

294
00:14:05,240 --> 00:14:08,320
they all had a
UNet-based architecture.

295
00:14:08,320 --> 00:14:11,690
And then until SDXL,
which is a fairly recent--

296
00:14:11,690 --> 00:14:15,260
I would like to say recent
because it's from 2023.

297
00:14:15,260 --> 00:14:18,213
And if you are trying to
develop a chronology of all

298
00:14:18,213 --> 00:14:19,880
the architectures
that have come around,

299
00:14:19,880 --> 00:14:23,020
I would like to argue that
SDXL is fairly decent.

300
00:14:23,020 --> 00:14:27,470
And also based on its
usage, I think it's decent.

301
00:14:27,470 --> 00:14:32,660
So until SDXL all the
works relied on UNet.

302
00:14:32,660 --> 00:14:35,500
So I think it makes
sense to discuss

303
00:14:35,500 --> 00:14:39,640
the UNet-based architecture
in this paradigm,

304
00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:43,750
and then start seeing
why we really need

305
00:14:43,750 --> 00:14:46,600
to transition to transformers.

306
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:49,750
So yeah.

307
00:14:49,750 --> 00:14:54,550
And of course, the UNet
for diffusion, it's giant.

308
00:14:54,550 --> 00:14:56,560
It's also one of the
reasons why you would

309
00:14:56,560 --> 00:14:58,250
want to probably get rid of it.

310
00:14:58,250 --> 00:15:00,380
But that's not the
end of the story.

311
00:15:00,380 --> 00:15:04,210
But let's try to see what are
the different components that

312
00:15:04,210 --> 00:15:06,890
are involved in our giant UNet.

313
00:15:06,890 --> 00:15:10,000
So you of course, need to
have an input convolutional

314
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,270
stem that directly
operates at the inputs that

315
00:15:13,270 --> 00:15:14,540
are coming at it.

316
00:15:14,540 --> 00:15:18,580
And then you have a bunch of
downblocks, which is basically

317
00:15:18,580 --> 00:15:22,150
comprised of custom
residual blocks made

318
00:15:22,150 --> 00:15:26,510
of normalization layers
and convolutional layers.

319
00:15:26,510 --> 00:15:28,780
And then you have got
custom transformer blocks.

320
00:15:28,780 --> 00:15:33,800
Again, normalization projections
and regular transformer blocks.

321
00:15:33,800 --> 00:15:36,080
And then you have got
convolutional layers

322
00:15:36,080 --> 00:15:37,260
for upsampling.

323
00:15:37,260 --> 00:15:40,130

324
00:15:40,130 --> 00:15:42,510
So when you are operating
on the latent space,

325
00:15:42,510 --> 00:15:45,270
you would want to upsample
to a higher resolution.

326
00:15:45,270 --> 00:15:48,380
And then you would
have some middle block

327
00:15:48,380 --> 00:15:54,380
like you would have in a
standard UNet architecture where

328
00:15:54,380 --> 00:15:56,760
you would not have any
resolution changes.

329
00:15:56,760 --> 00:16:01,010
And then you would have a series
of blocks which will basically

330
00:16:01,010 --> 00:16:06,450
downsample the upsampled outputs
that you had in your downblocks.

331
00:16:06,450 --> 00:16:08,930
And finally, you will
produce the output

332
00:16:08,930 --> 00:16:11,550
that will have the same
shape as your input.

333
00:16:11,550 --> 00:16:15,380
And it basically
resembles the same blocks

334
00:16:15,380 --> 00:16:19,950
that you would have in
your downblock counterpart.

335
00:16:19,950 --> 00:16:22,850
But instead of
doing upsampling, it

336
00:16:22,850 --> 00:16:25,680
will have downsampling layers.

337
00:16:25,680 --> 00:16:27,820
And there are some
miscellaneous things

338
00:16:27,820 --> 00:16:30,250
that you should worry about,
for example, time step

339
00:16:30,250 --> 00:16:33,170
embeddings-- how you should
embed your time steps,

340
00:16:33,170 --> 00:16:34,640
and additional embeddings.

341
00:16:34,640 --> 00:16:36,950
For example, if it's a
class conditional model,

342
00:16:36,950 --> 00:16:38,990
the way to embed them
would be different.

343
00:16:38,990 --> 00:16:42,560
If it's a tech like
text-to-image model,

344
00:16:42,560 --> 00:16:45,230
the way to embed the text
embeddings would be different.

345
00:16:45,230 --> 00:16:48,700
Basically, the way you modulate
all your conditional embeddings

346
00:16:48,700 --> 00:16:50,950
that will change depending
on the kind of conditions

347
00:16:50,950 --> 00:16:53,380
that you are dealing with.

348
00:16:53,380 --> 00:16:57,520
And here's basically how
a downblock of the UNet

349
00:16:57,520 --> 00:16:59,080
architecture looks like.

350
00:16:59,080 --> 00:17:01,880
It's so, so giant that
I had to transpose it.

351
00:17:01,880 --> 00:17:05,089
So it's very painful to
even think in the head.

352
00:17:05,089 --> 00:17:08,210
So if you are someone that works
with architectures quite a bit,

353
00:17:08,210 --> 00:17:11,720
I think we'll have to solemnly
agree that this is bad.

354
00:17:11,720 --> 00:17:13,310
This is bad news already.

355
00:17:13,310 --> 00:17:15,490
I can't even imagine
it in my head.

356
00:17:15,490 --> 00:17:20,170
And here's a bit
of a zoomed in look

357
00:17:20,170 --> 00:17:25,010
of what goes into that
downblock component of the UNet.

358
00:17:25,010 --> 00:17:27,010
You have got a bunch
of custom ResNet blocks

359
00:17:27,010 --> 00:17:28,520
as I was discussing.

360
00:17:28,520 --> 00:17:31,120
I've got a bunch of
custom transformer blocks

361
00:17:31,120 --> 00:17:33,320
as I was discussing,
but nothing too brutal.

362
00:17:33,320 --> 00:17:35,750
Nothing that you
haven't seen already.

363
00:17:35,750 --> 00:17:38,780
So it's just a bunch of
composition of those blocks.

364
00:17:38,780 --> 00:17:41,890
And I have also tried mentioning
the resolution changes

365
00:17:41,890 --> 00:17:43,713
in each of the stages.

366
00:17:43,713 --> 00:17:46,480

367
00:17:46,480 --> 00:17:49,700
And putting it all together,
it looks something like this.

368
00:17:49,700 --> 00:17:52,450
I mean, I have tried
shortening it quite a bit,

369
00:17:52,450 --> 00:17:55,270
but if I were to
imagine it in my head

370
00:17:55,270 --> 00:17:56,890
it's going to be
extremely painful.

371
00:17:56,890 --> 00:17:58,990
And the blocks are just--

372
00:17:58,990 --> 00:17:59,660
damn.

373
00:17:59,660 --> 00:18:01,540
It's very prohibitive
in nature just

374
00:18:01,540 --> 00:18:04,330
for the task of
image generation.

375
00:18:04,330 --> 00:18:05,270
So yeah.

376
00:18:05,270 --> 00:18:07,550
That's the complete perspective.

377
00:18:07,550 --> 00:18:11,180
I know it's not as complete
as you might expect,

378
00:18:11,180 --> 00:18:15,770
but it's also hard to fit all
the UNet in a single screen.

379
00:18:15,770 --> 00:18:18,250
So yeah.

380
00:18:18,250 --> 00:18:21,670
And then of course,
the natural next thing

381
00:18:21,670 --> 00:18:24,970
that one would
have tried would be

382
00:18:24,970 --> 00:18:27,670
to try to replace the
convolutional layers with MLP

383
00:18:27,670 --> 00:18:29,900
blocks, to try to
simplify quite a bit.

384
00:18:29,900 --> 00:18:32,290
And yeah, folks
from Google tried it

385
00:18:32,290 --> 00:18:34,520
in the UViT architecture.

386
00:18:34,520 --> 00:18:36,556
So that's there.

387
00:18:36,556 --> 00:18:41,500
That was the precursor to
the pure transformer-based

388
00:18:41,500 --> 00:18:42,980
architectures, but not quite.

389
00:18:42,980 --> 00:18:46,580
It still had its fair
share of complications,

390
00:18:46,580 --> 00:18:50,650
and the architectural
painfulness as you

391
00:18:50,650 --> 00:18:52,335
saw in the UNet-based design.

392
00:18:52,335 --> 00:18:54,910

393
00:18:54,910 --> 00:18:59,080
Now, I'm going to now try
to motivate why we really

394
00:18:59,080 --> 00:19:01,850
need a pure transformer-based
architecture.

395
00:19:01,850 --> 00:19:04,670
Now, the first point is
probably very obvious.

396
00:19:04,670 --> 00:19:07,630
We would want to benefit
from the advancements that

397
00:19:07,630 --> 00:19:09,945
are happening in the
transformer-based architectures,

398
00:19:09,945 --> 00:19:12,610
like all the divine
benevolence, as Noam Shazeer

399
00:19:12,610 --> 00:19:14,260
like likes to call it.

400
00:19:14,260 --> 00:19:15,830
You would want to
have some gelu,

401
00:19:15,830 --> 00:19:17,780
you would want to have
quick normalization,

402
00:19:17,780 --> 00:19:20,510
you would want to have
parallel MLP layers and so on.

403
00:19:20,510 --> 00:19:22,000
So that's one.

404
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:25,100
Of course, good scaling
properties ee and so on.

405
00:19:25,100 --> 00:19:27,700
And then let's
say would you want

406
00:19:27,700 --> 00:19:31,630
to connect the pure
transformer-based diffusion

407
00:19:31,630 --> 00:19:36,080
architecture or the backbone,
with some other backbones,

408
00:19:36,080 --> 00:19:38,300
let's say a pure
LLM-based backbone.

409
00:19:38,300 --> 00:19:40,430
The integration
becomes very easy.

410
00:19:40,430 --> 00:19:43,210
And then it allows
you to get rid

411
00:19:43,210 --> 00:19:47,060
of the giant UNet, which I
guess is my main motivation.

412
00:19:47,060 --> 00:19:51,250
But I hope I was able to
convince you fairly strongly why

413
00:19:51,250 --> 00:19:54,970
we need a change--

414
00:19:54,970 --> 00:19:58,570
a paradigm shift in the
architectures that are primarily

415
00:19:58,570 --> 00:20:00,900
inspired from the UNet design.

416
00:20:00,900 --> 00:20:03,490

417
00:20:03,490 --> 00:20:07,250
And also, this is sight
to the sour eyes already.

418
00:20:07,250 --> 00:20:08,680
This is not uncommon.

419
00:20:08,680 --> 00:20:11,590
We all that this is
the standard forward

420
00:20:11,590 --> 00:20:13,880
pass in a vision
transformer network.

421
00:20:13,880 --> 00:20:17,260
They should feel very familiar
at this point in time.

422
00:20:17,260 --> 00:20:19,790
Now, the point I am
trying to make here

423
00:20:19,790 --> 00:20:23,660
is this doesn't have to
change a whole lot if we

424
00:20:23,660 --> 00:20:27,330
were to extrapolate this
to image generation.

425
00:20:27,330 --> 00:20:30,710
And I think you will agree
with me that this is not

426
00:20:30,710 --> 00:20:31,970
changing a lot.

427
00:20:31,970 --> 00:20:34,580
All the core
components are there,

428
00:20:34,580 --> 00:20:39,810
the patchification is there, the
positional embeddings are there.

429
00:20:39,810 --> 00:20:43,190
Of course, the way to
class embed things, that's

430
00:20:43,190 --> 00:20:45,230
different-- the y embedder bit.

431
00:20:45,230 --> 00:20:47,180
You would still need
to have a component

432
00:20:47,180 --> 00:20:48,840
to embed your time steps--

433
00:20:48,840 --> 00:20:49,680
that's different.

434
00:20:49,680 --> 00:20:52,020
But the rest of the
components is still there.

435
00:20:52,020 --> 00:20:54,510
You are still iterating
through the blocks.

436
00:20:54,510 --> 00:20:57,110
You have your final
layer to finally decode

437
00:20:57,110 --> 00:21:00,450
your outputs and so on, and
the non-patchification layer.

438
00:21:00,450 --> 00:21:04,430
So this is still very similar
to how you would do it

439
00:21:04,430 --> 00:21:06,180
in a standard width.

440
00:21:06,180 --> 00:21:11,990
But of course, you have to you
account for the generation head

441
00:21:11,990 --> 00:21:13,500
also at the same time.

442
00:21:13,500 --> 00:21:16,190
So my point is you
are not changing

443
00:21:16,190 --> 00:21:21,560
a whole lot in the standard
width forward pass.

444
00:21:21,560 --> 00:21:23,720
Now, taking a
closer look, I think

445
00:21:23,720 --> 00:21:29,060
this should also feel very
familiar, apart from a few mods

446
00:21:29,060 --> 00:21:29,790
here and there.

447
00:21:29,790 --> 00:21:32,840
We have got some scale and
shift parameters, which

448
00:21:32,840 --> 00:21:34,950
I'm going to discuss in a bit.

449
00:21:34,950 --> 00:21:36,570
But the rest of
the other blocks,

450
00:21:36,570 --> 00:21:39,800
you have got the same layer now,
you have got the patchification

451
00:21:39,800 --> 00:21:41,300
layer, you have
got some embeddings

452
00:21:41,300 --> 00:21:44,580
and so on, and then you have got
a linear and reshape operation.

453
00:21:44,580 --> 00:21:47,790
So most of it should
feel familiar.

454
00:21:47,790 --> 00:21:51,725
But the ones that are
apparently a little foreign,

455
00:21:51,725 --> 00:21:52,975
I'm going to discuss them now.

456
00:21:52,975 --> 00:21:55,520

457
00:21:55,520 --> 00:21:57,530
Let's start with
the time step bit.

458
00:21:57,530 --> 00:21:59,820
How do we actually
embed time steps?

459
00:21:59,820 --> 00:22:03,720
I've been talking about time
steps for quite a bit now.

460
00:22:03,720 --> 00:22:07,620
But let's now see how do we
actually embed the time steps.

461
00:22:07,620 --> 00:22:10,020
And time steps are
really important.

462
00:22:10,020 --> 00:22:13,520
And I'm also going to show
you the expected shapes,

463
00:22:13,520 --> 00:22:14,580
the output shapes.

464
00:22:14,580 --> 00:22:16,320
And in this case,
this is batch size,

465
00:22:16,320 --> 00:22:18,800
comma, the hidden dimension
of the transformer

466
00:22:18,800 --> 00:22:21,560
blocks that you are expecting.

467
00:22:21,560 --> 00:22:23,540
So how do we embed time steps?

468
00:22:23,540 --> 00:22:26,940
And time steps can range
in between 0 to 1,000,

469
00:22:26,940 --> 00:22:30,890
where 0 meaning like no noise,
and 1,000 should mean it's fully

470
00:22:30,890 --> 00:22:33,110
noised.

471
00:22:33,110 --> 00:22:37,190
So each t is embedded
into sinusoidal frequency

472
00:22:37,190 --> 00:22:39,240
to make them phase aware.

473
00:22:39,240 --> 00:22:41,840
At any point in
time, the network

474
00:22:41,840 --> 00:22:44,540
is seeing extremely low
frequencies and extremely high

475
00:22:44,540 --> 00:22:45,360
frequencies.

476
00:22:45,360 --> 00:22:49,680
And it must be aware
of the kind of phase

477
00:22:49,680 --> 00:22:51,690
it should be modulating into.

478
00:22:51,690 --> 00:22:54,960
So that's why sinusoidal
frequencies are really helpful.

479
00:22:54,960 --> 00:22:58,790
And then after that, how
the network should weigh

480
00:22:58,790 --> 00:23:00,180
these different frequencies.

481
00:23:00,180 --> 00:23:02,520
And in order to
model those weights,

482
00:23:02,520 --> 00:23:07,340
we basically pass it
through a very shallow MLP.

483
00:23:07,340 --> 00:23:09,480
And then how do you
embed class labels?

484
00:23:09,480 --> 00:23:12,530
You just take an
nn.Embedding layer--

485
00:23:12,530 --> 00:23:13,800
as simple as that.

486
00:23:13,800 --> 00:23:15,660
And this is the standard
patchification--

487
00:23:15,660 --> 00:23:18,090
you do it with a
convolutional stem.

488
00:23:18,090 --> 00:23:20,390
And for positional
encodings they

489
00:23:20,390 --> 00:23:25,340
use the standard
sine-cosine scheme.

490
00:23:25,340 --> 00:23:29,720
And the final conditioning is
you first embed the time steps

491
00:23:29,720 --> 00:23:31,590
and then you embed
the class labels,

492
00:23:31,590 --> 00:23:33,240
and you basically sum them up.

493
00:23:33,240 --> 00:23:36,050
And then you have
your final condition

494
00:23:36,050 --> 00:23:38,280
that goes into the
transformer blocks.

495
00:23:38,280 --> 00:23:40,790
And this is very important
to note that c remains

496
00:23:40,790 --> 00:23:43,170
fixed across all the blocks.

497
00:23:43,170 --> 00:23:44,770
So that's very
important to note.

498
00:23:44,770 --> 00:23:47,510

499
00:23:47,510 --> 00:23:52,490
And you would probably
think the conditioning--

500
00:23:52,490 --> 00:23:54,680
in order to model
the conditioning

501
00:23:54,680 --> 00:23:57,530
with the actual inputs,
the noisy latency

502
00:23:57,530 --> 00:24:00,600
would probably want to
use cross-attention,

503
00:24:00,600 --> 00:24:07,380
but that's not the case, as we
will see in a few slides later.

504
00:24:07,380 --> 00:24:09,770
So let's take a
step back and try

505
00:24:09,770 --> 00:24:15,560
to think of how we can
inject the conditioning bit

506
00:24:15,560 --> 00:24:18,230
into the transformer blocks.

507
00:24:18,230 --> 00:24:22,730
So we have something called
adaptive layer-norm, which

508
00:24:22,730 --> 00:24:26,600
is very important in
order to be able to model

509
00:24:26,600 --> 00:24:30,000
the stylistic aspects that you
are getting out of your images.

510
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:31,830
And it's basically this--

511
00:24:31,830 --> 00:24:33,990
so you have your
standard layer norm,

512
00:24:33,990 --> 00:24:36,770
and then you have
an additional set

513
00:24:36,770 --> 00:24:41,630
of parameters which we call
modulation parameters, which

514
00:24:41,630 --> 00:24:43,740
basically operate on
the condition space.

515
00:24:43,740 --> 00:24:46,280
And remember, the
condition is basically

516
00:24:46,280 --> 00:24:48,860
a summation of the
time step embeddings,

517
00:24:48,860 --> 00:24:51,030
as well as the class embeddings.

518
00:24:51,030 --> 00:24:55,250
So that's your
condition right there.

519
00:24:55,250 --> 00:24:59,510
And skipping the regular
transformer bits like the QKV,

520
00:24:59,510 --> 00:25:03,170
the multi-head, self-attention
and the MLP layers--

521
00:25:03,170 --> 00:25:06,890
we the equations that
govern the computations

522
00:25:06,890 --> 00:25:09,410
that take place within a
standard transformer encoder

523
00:25:09,410 --> 00:25:10,010
block.

524
00:25:10,010 --> 00:25:12,260
So this is fairly well-known.

525
00:25:12,260 --> 00:25:15,080
And the part that's not
known at this point in time

526
00:25:15,080 --> 00:25:19,800
hopefully is how do we
modulate the conditioning bit.

527
00:25:19,800 --> 00:25:23,270
How do we actually inject the
conditioning in the transformer

528
00:25:23,270 --> 00:25:23,820
blocks?

529
00:25:23,820 --> 00:25:25,560
So this is how we do it.

530
00:25:25,560 --> 00:25:27,290
Instead of doing
any cross-attention

531
00:25:27,290 --> 00:25:30,530
which would have been a fairly
natural choice, I guess.

532
00:25:30,530 --> 00:25:32,790
We actually do not
do cross-attention.

533
00:25:32,790 --> 00:25:34,920
We are still doing
self-attention,

534
00:25:34,920 --> 00:25:38,270
and then we are basically
modulating the conditioning

535
00:25:38,270 --> 00:25:39,800
along with
self-attention, as you

536
00:25:39,800 --> 00:25:44,240
can see in the bottom
half of your equations.

537
00:25:44,240 --> 00:25:48,020
And these modulation parameters
are learned from the modality

538
00:25:48,020 --> 00:25:49,700
that you are training
these things on.

539
00:25:49,700 --> 00:25:53,240

540
00:25:53,240 --> 00:25:56,970
And then in order to
get your final outputs,

541
00:25:56,970 --> 00:25:59,600
you basically have a
single layer decoder.

542
00:25:59,600 --> 00:26:02,150
And then you
basically unpatchify

543
00:26:02,150 --> 00:26:08,300
it to get the same
shape as your inputs.

544
00:26:08,300 --> 00:26:12,650
Now, a note on init,
because the initialization

545
00:26:12,650 --> 00:26:16,970
is fairly important-- it's all
standard vision transformer,

546
00:26:16,970 --> 00:26:20,220
init, but with two
key modifications.

547
00:26:20,220 --> 00:26:25,170
Each transformer block is
initialized as identity block,

548
00:26:25,170 --> 00:26:28,040
taking inspiration from
the early works in ImageNet

549
00:26:28,040 --> 00:26:31,860
training, wherein if you have
a bunch of ResNet blocks,

550
00:26:31,860 --> 00:26:34,430
you usually initialize the
beta parameter in the batch

551
00:26:34,430 --> 00:26:35,940
normalization layer as 0.

552
00:26:35,940 --> 00:26:38,700
It helps with training
stability, and stabilization.

553
00:26:38,700 --> 00:26:41,870
And it turns out that's
the case here as well.

554
00:26:41,870 --> 00:26:46,110
Now, coming to the adaptive
layer normalization thing.

555
00:26:46,110 --> 00:26:50,630
It's very important and it's
also more compute efficient.

556
00:26:50,630 --> 00:26:53,300
And as I was mentioning,
cross-attention

557
00:26:53,300 --> 00:26:56,000
would have been the
natural choice in order

558
00:26:56,000 --> 00:27:00,380
to model the dependency between
the conditions and the noise

559
00:27:00,380 --> 00:27:01,170
inputs.

560
00:27:01,170 --> 00:27:05,220
But that's not at all the case
if you take a look at the graph.

561
00:27:05,220 --> 00:27:07,550
In fact, adaptive layer-norm
performs the best,

562
00:27:07,550 --> 00:27:10,080
and it beats
cross-attention big time.

563
00:27:10,080 --> 00:27:12,920
And it's also not because
the conditions are fairly

564
00:27:12,920 --> 00:27:17,150
simple to model, but it's
because when you are operating

565
00:27:17,150 --> 00:27:20,850
with continuous
modalities like images,

566
00:27:20,850 --> 00:27:24,110
it's not that trivial
to model dependencies

567
00:27:24,110 --> 00:27:25,230
with cross-attention.

568
00:27:25,230 --> 00:27:29,550
And when your conditions are
simple like class embeddings,

569
00:27:29,550 --> 00:27:33,150
it doesn't make sense to model
them with cross-attention.

570
00:27:33,150 --> 00:27:36,050
It's also because it's
a waste of compute,

571
00:27:36,050 --> 00:27:40,520
and the graph confirms it.

572
00:27:40,520 --> 00:27:42,500
And later works
have also explored

573
00:27:42,500 --> 00:27:46,310
a more compute efficient variant
of adaptive layer-norm, which

574
00:27:46,310 --> 00:27:49,370
we are going to get to
in a couple slides later.

575
00:27:49,370 --> 00:27:52,850
And how you are modulating
these conditions--

576
00:27:52,850 --> 00:27:55,220
in this case, we are
basically operating

577
00:27:55,220 --> 00:27:58,580
on a summation of the time
step embeddings and the class

578
00:27:58,580 --> 00:27:59,370
embeddings.

579
00:27:59,370 --> 00:28:03,140
Now, how you are modulating
it across and throughout

580
00:28:03,140 --> 00:28:04,950
your different
transformer blocks,

581
00:28:04,950 --> 00:28:07,160
that becomes very
important, as we will see

582
00:28:07,160 --> 00:28:10,700
in a couple of blocks later.

583
00:28:10,700 --> 00:28:14,090
And as expected, it
scales fairly graciously

584
00:28:14,090 --> 00:28:15,180
with more compute.

585
00:28:15,180 --> 00:28:17,450
And it turns out that
you can basically

586
00:28:17,450 --> 00:28:21,860
apply all the UNet-based
training techniques

587
00:28:21,860 --> 00:28:24,960
to a diffusion transformer.

588
00:28:24,960 --> 00:28:26,280
So that's pretty cool.

589
00:28:26,280 --> 00:28:29,210
It scales pretty graciously.

590
00:28:29,210 --> 00:28:32,540
Performs also well
when compared to other

591
00:28:32,540 --> 00:28:34,866
equally sized unit counterparts.

592
00:28:34,866 --> 00:28:38,750

593
00:28:38,750 --> 00:28:41,100
And of course, at
this point in time,

594
00:28:41,100 --> 00:28:42,920
you must be thinking
no one really

595
00:28:42,920 --> 00:28:45,960
does class conditional in the
space of image generation.

596
00:28:45,960 --> 00:28:50,870
So yeah, that's where
we are headed next.

597
00:28:50,870 --> 00:28:56,540
Now, I want to try
to motivate what

598
00:28:56,540 --> 00:29:00,230
it would take to enable
text-to-image generation

599
00:29:00,230 --> 00:29:03,760
in the standard diffusion
transformer architecture,

600
00:29:03,760 --> 00:29:06,390
because I think it
makes sense to approach

601
00:29:06,390 --> 00:29:08,220
the problem in that sense.

602
00:29:08,220 --> 00:29:11,970
What are the components that are
missing in a standard diffusion

603
00:29:11,970 --> 00:29:14,280
transformer, so that
it becomes a tool

604
00:29:14,280 --> 00:29:16,150
for text-to-image
generation as well?

605
00:29:16,150 --> 00:29:19,320
And I think PixArt-alpha is
one of the early works that

606
00:29:19,320 --> 00:29:20,640
explored it.

607
00:29:20,640 --> 00:29:25,470
So yeah, we are going to
definitely see it in details.

608
00:29:25,470 --> 00:29:28,170
Now, one natural
question would be

609
00:29:28,170 --> 00:29:32,380
how to embed the input
natural language text prompts.

610
00:29:32,380 --> 00:29:34,090
And the answer would be simple.

611
00:29:34,090 --> 00:29:36,370
You will need a text encoder.

612
00:29:36,370 --> 00:29:41,310
And that's exactly what
the PixArt-alpha work does.

613
00:29:41,310 --> 00:29:45,240
And then how to learn your
contextual dependencies.

614
00:29:45,240 --> 00:29:48,940
Now we have instead of
class instead of classes,

615
00:29:48,940 --> 00:29:53,850
we have got natural language
text on top of time steps.

616
00:29:53,850 --> 00:29:57,130
We could do self-attention
on noisy latents,

617
00:29:57,130 --> 00:29:59,340
and then we could
do cross-attention

618
00:29:59,340 --> 00:30:01,540
in between your noisy
latents and text.

619
00:30:01,540 --> 00:30:04,450
And mind you, this
text is not just class.

620
00:30:04,450 --> 00:30:07,290
This text is some natural
language description,

621
00:30:07,290 --> 00:30:11,350
like a baby astronaut hatching
out of an egg on the moon.

622
00:30:11,350 --> 00:30:13,235
So this is natural
language description

623
00:30:13,235 --> 00:30:14,110
we are talking about.

624
00:30:14,110 --> 00:30:16,800
This is not simply class labels.

625
00:30:16,800 --> 00:30:19,230
So that could make sense.

626
00:30:19,230 --> 00:30:23,110
It could make sense to have
self-attention on noisy latents

627
00:30:23,110 --> 00:30:26,530
to model the local dependencies
within the patches,

628
00:30:26,530 --> 00:30:31,290
and then also do cross-attention
in between the noisy patches

629
00:30:31,290 --> 00:30:33,690
and the text embeddings.

630
00:30:33,690 --> 00:30:35,910
And then we'll have
to figure a way out

631
00:30:35,910 --> 00:30:37,600
to modulate the
time steps as well.

632
00:30:37,600 --> 00:30:40,200
And as we saw in the
DiT work that it's

633
00:30:40,200 --> 00:30:42,870
important to modulate
the time step

634
00:30:42,870 --> 00:30:47,160
embeddings throughout
your transformer blocks.

635
00:30:47,160 --> 00:30:51,540
And then if you have got access
to a class conditional diffusion

636
00:30:51,540 --> 00:30:53,560
transformer, and
if it's compatible,

637
00:30:53,560 --> 00:30:58,570
it might also make sense to
initialize some blocks from it.

638
00:30:58,570 --> 00:31:01,560
Because why waste compute?

639
00:31:01,560 --> 00:31:05,375
It might help with training
stabilization and so on.

640
00:31:05,375 --> 00:31:08,610

641
00:31:08,610 --> 00:31:11,800
Now, that's exactly
what PixArt-alpha does.

642
00:31:11,800 --> 00:31:16,540
So if you were paying attention
at this point in time,

643
00:31:16,540 --> 00:31:20,700
you would have realized that
implementing these things

644
00:31:20,700 --> 00:31:24,810
is not extremely challenging.

645
00:31:24,810 --> 00:31:28,900
But it helps to know that that's
exactly what PixArt-alpha does.

646
00:31:28,900 --> 00:31:32,320
So it uses a text encoder
to embed the prompts.

647
00:31:32,320 --> 00:31:35,140
It uses self-attention
on noisy latents.

648
00:31:35,140 --> 00:31:38,280
It uses cross-attention
to model the dependency

649
00:31:38,280 --> 00:31:41,920
between the noisy latents
and the text embeddings.

650
00:31:41,920 --> 00:31:45,750
And it also initializes from
a class conditional DiT model

651
00:31:45,750 --> 00:31:47,550
in order to accelerate training.

652
00:31:47,550 --> 00:31:52,080
So it helps to that you can
still think about these things

653
00:31:52,080 --> 00:31:54,590
and see it getting
implemented in practice.

654
00:31:54,590 --> 00:31:57,120

655
00:31:57,120 --> 00:32:01,750
And here's some discussion
around the use of text encoder.

656
00:32:01,750 --> 00:32:07,620
So PixArt-alpha used
Flan-T5-xxl in order

657
00:32:07,620 --> 00:32:10,510
to really get that
text-rendering ability.

658
00:32:10,510 --> 00:32:13,480
And some of the concurrent
works like Imagine,

659
00:32:13,480 --> 00:32:16,660
they showed that if you
scale the text encoder,

660
00:32:16,660 --> 00:32:20,320
and if you ever wanted to
render text in your images,

661
00:32:20,320 --> 00:32:22,840
having a better text
encoder actually helps.

662
00:32:22,840 --> 00:32:25,050
And also, then
there's this problem

663
00:32:25,050 --> 00:32:27,760
of long prompt
following ability,

664
00:32:27,760 --> 00:32:33,190
because models like SDXL, they
rely on clip for embedding text.

665
00:32:33,190 --> 00:32:35,320
And CLIP has a very
short context length.

666
00:32:35,320 --> 00:32:36,850
I think it's 77.

667
00:32:36,850 --> 00:32:41,140
But with the T5-xxl, you get
a way longer context length.

668
00:32:41,140 --> 00:32:44,740
So you basically get
to have longer prompts.

669
00:32:44,740 --> 00:32:48,540
You get to describe your
prompt in a bit more detail.

670
00:32:48,540 --> 00:32:49,900
So that's there.

671
00:32:49,900 --> 00:32:52,840
And also exploring the
space of text encoder,

672
00:32:52,840 --> 00:32:56,650
it's still a good research
problem to take a look at.

673
00:32:56,650 --> 00:32:58,500
And if you were
wondering, why not

674
00:32:58,500 --> 00:33:02,260
just use a standard language
model in place of T5?

675
00:33:02,260 --> 00:33:04,740
Well, it's not that
difficult to actually use it.

676
00:33:04,740 --> 00:33:09,040
At many works like Lumina,
they explore it quite a bit.

677
00:33:09,040 --> 00:33:11,130
And if you were wondering,
diffusion models

678
00:33:11,130 --> 00:33:12,490
are already compute bound.

679
00:33:12,490 --> 00:33:16,570
So why add the baggage of
adding another heavy model?

680
00:33:16,570 --> 00:33:20,280
Well, I think it's OK to
use that because computing

681
00:33:20,280 --> 00:33:22,810
prompt embedding it's
a one step process.

682
00:33:22,810 --> 00:33:25,350
So it's OK to have
a memory bound model

683
00:33:25,350 --> 00:33:30,060
like a large language
model in the mix.

684
00:33:30,060 --> 00:33:31,290
Cool.

685
00:33:31,290 --> 00:33:37,380
And here, we will again see the
return of adaptive layer-norm.

686
00:33:37,380 --> 00:33:40,980
And remember if you
forgot, remember

687
00:33:40,980 --> 00:33:43,870
that for each diffusion
transformer block,

688
00:33:43,870 --> 00:33:47,460
we were operating on a summation
of time step embeddings

689
00:33:47,460 --> 00:33:50,110
and class embeddings.

690
00:33:50,110 --> 00:33:53,820

691
00:33:53,820 --> 00:33:58,320
For PixArt-alpha, we
already have a way

692
00:33:58,320 --> 00:34:01,600
to compute our text embeddings,
which we do not want to touch,

693
00:34:01,600 --> 00:34:03,240
because those
embeddings are already

694
00:34:03,240 --> 00:34:07,360
computed with a dedicated
rich text encoder.

695
00:34:07,360 --> 00:34:10,080
So we maybe do not want
to touch those embeddings

696
00:34:10,080 --> 00:34:11,170
and modulate them.

697
00:34:11,170 --> 00:34:14,969
Unlike diffusion transformers,
where we modulated

698
00:34:14,969 --> 00:34:16,510
the class embeddings as well.

699
00:34:16,510 --> 00:34:20,969
And instead of having
adaptive layer normalization

700
00:34:20,969 --> 00:34:25,810
blocks in every diffusion
transformer encoder block,

701
00:34:25,810 --> 00:34:27,460
we basically maintain tables.

702
00:34:27,460 --> 00:34:31,960
We basically maintain embedding
tables and we sum them up.

703
00:34:31,960 --> 00:34:34,320
So instead of doing
another matmul

704
00:34:34,320 --> 00:34:38,560
for each and every
transformer encoder block,

705
00:34:38,560 --> 00:34:41,340
we basically get
away with addition.

706
00:34:41,340 --> 00:34:44,530
Addition with another
embedding table.

707
00:34:44,530 --> 00:34:47,590
And it helps us reduce
the compute quite a bit.

708
00:34:47,590 --> 00:34:51,929
It helps us reduce 27% of the
original diffusion transformer

709
00:34:51,929 --> 00:34:52,989
computation.

710
00:34:52,989 --> 00:34:55,920
And I think the idea
is fairly elegant.

711
00:34:55,920 --> 00:34:58,830
It also gives
another perspective

712
00:34:58,830 --> 00:35:02,370
to think about how you can
reduce parameters and still

713
00:35:02,370 --> 00:35:04,320
maintain performance.

714
00:35:04,320 --> 00:35:07,240
So this was good.

715
00:35:07,240 --> 00:35:07,740
Cool.

716
00:35:07,740 --> 00:35:10,380
And I must say this is
impressive performance

717
00:35:10,380 --> 00:35:11,980
for a fairly compact model.

718
00:35:11,980 --> 00:35:14,770
It's only 0.6 billion
parameter model.

719
00:35:14,770 --> 00:35:17,980
And it's already
breaking all the charts.

720
00:35:17,980 --> 00:35:24,720
When it came around in
2023, it was fairly good.

721
00:35:24,720 --> 00:35:27,640
I think I have the
[? Geneva ?] scores.

722
00:35:27,640 --> 00:35:29,250
No, we don't.

723
00:35:29,250 --> 00:35:31,590
But the last chart
here, it basically

724
00:35:31,590 --> 00:35:34,500
shows you the human
preference rating.

725
00:35:34,500 --> 00:35:37,410
Quality wise, it's
overall image quality.

726
00:35:37,410 --> 00:35:40,110
And alignment wise,
it's the alignment

727
00:35:40,110 --> 00:35:42,820
between the text and
the generated images.

728
00:35:42,820 --> 00:35:46,500
And in both of those
aspects, PixArt-alpha

729
00:35:46,500 --> 00:35:47,585
performed fairly well.

730
00:35:47,585 --> 00:35:51,060

731
00:35:51,060 --> 00:35:59,850
Now, before I jump to the time
and memory complexity of these

732
00:35:59,850 --> 00:36:03,660
models-- because we are still
using the quadratic vanilla

733
00:36:03,660 --> 00:36:04,960
attention thing--

734
00:36:04,960 --> 00:36:08,640
I want to see if there are any
questions at this point in time.

735
00:36:08,640 --> 00:36:11,640
So I'm going to open the floor
for questions if that's OK,

736
00:36:11,640 --> 00:36:12,750
Stephen?

737
00:36:12,750 --> 00:36:13,300
That's OK.

738
00:36:13,300 --> 00:36:16,480

739
00:36:16,480 --> 00:36:16,980
Yeah.

740
00:36:16,980 --> 00:36:18,690
Go ahead.

741
00:36:18,690 --> 00:36:22,860
You mentioned that the state
of the art diffusion models

742
00:36:22,860 --> 00:36:25,570
rely on more than
one text encoder.

743
00:36:25,570 --> 00:36:26,880
Why is that?

744
00:36:26,880 --> 00:36:30,300
Or what's the benefit of
having multiple different types

745
00:36:30,300 --> 00:36:33,180
of encodings for the same text?

746
00:36:33,180 --> 00:36:33,790
Oh yeah.

747
00:36:33,790 --> 00:36:34,350
For sure.

748
00:36:34,350 --> 00:36:36,270
So the question
is, why would you

749
00:36:36,270 --> 00:36:39,120
want to have multiple
text encoders,

750
00:36:39,120 --> 00:36:43,480
and why does it help improve the
image generation performance?

751
00:36:43,480 --> 00:36:47,230
So you saw in class
conditional DiTs,

752
00:36:47,230 --> 00:36:50,140
you modulate not just
the time step embeddings,

753
00:36:50,140 --> 00:36:52,950
but also the class embeddings.

754
00:36:52,950 --> 00:36:56,580
So the conditional embeddings
that we were modulating, along

755
00:36:56,580 --> 00:36:59,640
with self-attention were a
summation of class embeddings

756
00:36:59,640 --> 00:37:00,760
and time step embeddings.

757
00:37:00,760 --> 00:37:05,800
Now, for text-to-image models,
that is not as trivial.

758
00:37:05,800 --> 00:37:07,830
So apart from your
text embeddings,

759
00:37:07,830 --> 00:37:11,230
you also have your
time step embeddings.

760
00:37:11,230 --> 00:37:16,800
But what about modulating
the other condition that you

761
00:37:16,800 --> 00:37:19,090
computed from your text input?

762
00:37:19,090 --> 00:37:22,180
So turns out that the richer
the representations are,

763
00:37:22,180 --> 00:37:24,520
the diverse the
representations are,

764
00:37:24,520 --> 00:37:27,580
the better it is for
the generation backbone.

765
00:37:27,580 --> 00:37:33,460
Now, when I said many models do
use more than one text encoders,

766
00:37:33,460 --> 00:37:36,310
they usually have a
combination of CLIP and T5.

767
00:37:36,310 --> 00:37:38,130
Now CLIP is an entirely
different model

768
00:37:38,130 --> 00:37:39,970
and T5 is an entirely
different model.

769
00:37:39,970 --> 00:37:43,050
With CLIP, you embed some
kind of contrastive nature

770
00:37:43,050 --> 00:37:45,880
in the text embeddings,
and with T5 you

771
00:37:45,880 --> 00:37:50,750
have a completely different
nature in your text embeddings.

772
00:37:50,750 --> 00:37:54,110
So the more the diverse
are these embeddings,

773
00:37:54,110 --> 00:37:57,290
the better it is for the
generative performance.

774
00:37:57,290 --> 00:38:00,020
But there is no
systematic study of this.

775
00:38:00,020 --> 00:38:03,330
That's what the
general belief is.

776
00:38:03,330 --> 00:38:03,830
Yeah.

777
00:38:03,830 --> 00:38:07,990
But there are works that
get away just by using

778
00:38:07,990 --> 00:38:09,075
a single language model.

779
00:38:09,075 --> 00:38:12,310

780
00:38:12,310 --> 00:38:15,400
Maybe the language models
inherit both as a virtue

781
00:38:15,400 --> 00:38:17,950
of their good pre-training.

782
00:38:17,950 --> 00:38:19,720
Yeah.

783
00:38:19,720 --> 00:38:22,870
Any more questions?

784
00:38:22,870 --> 00:38:23,635
Yeah.

785
00:38:23,635 --> 00:38:25,930
Do most current
cutting-edge companies

786
00:38:25,930 --> 00:38:29,830
use diffusion transformers
now, or are some still using

787
00:38:29,830 --> 00:38:32,120
the original UNet
with convolutions,

788
00:38:32,120 --> 00:38:34,180
or is it just dependent
on the use case?

789
00:38:34,180 --> 00:38:37,690
I think most of them are using
diffusion transformers because

790
00:38:37,690 --> 00:38:41,090
of their efficiencies, and
also they are easy to adapt.

791
00:38:41,090 --> 00:38:44,750
If you wanted to embed
another form of control--

792
00:38:44,750 --> 00:38:48,910
let's say you wanted
to additionally prompt

793
00:38:48,910 --> 00:38:51,770
the model with some stylistic
reference from images,

794
00:38:51,770 --> 00:38:53,980
it's easier to do that on
a diffusion transformer,

795
00:38:53,980 --> 00:38:56,355
as you would, as we will see
in a couple of slides later.

796
00:38:56,355 --> 00:38:59,230

797
00:38:59,230 --> 00:39:02,500
How do you exactly avoid
the matrix multiplication?

798
00:39:02,500 --> 00:39:03,050
Oh yeah.

799
00:39:03,050 --> 00:39:03,953
Sure.

800
00:39:03,953 --> 00:39:05,120
Can you expand a little bit?

801
00:39:05,120 --> 00:39:05,960
Yeah, sure.

802
00:39:05,960 --> 00:39:12,820

803
00:39:12,820 --> 00:39:16,460
In the original diffusion
transformer block,

804
00:39:16,460 --> 00:39:19,180
you initialize all your
modulation parameters

805
00:39:19,180 --> 00:39:21,290
as basically an affine layer.

806
00:39:21,290 --> 00:39:23,440
So you can do that.

807
00:39:23,440 --> 00:39:26,470
You can chunk it and then you
have your different modulation

808
00:39:26,470 --> 00:39:29,290
parameters.

809
00:39:29,290 --> 00:39:34,370
And this is where your
matrix multiplication lies.

810
00:39:34,370 --> 00:39:39,536
And notice how I am computing
the modulation parameters.

811
00:39:39,536 --> 00:39:43,070
But here I am only computing
it through an embedding table.

812
00:39:43,070 --> 00:39:47,380
There's no affine
transformation happening.

813
00:39:47,380 --> 00:39:50,330
And I end up adding it to
my time step embeddings.

814
00:39:50,330 --> 00:39:52,120
That's it.

815
00:39:52,120 --> 00:39:56,020
So that's how I
avoid the matmul.

816
00:39:56,020 --> 00:40:00,130
And that's largely why
I am able to reduce

817
00:40:00,130 --> 00:40:04,000
the computation by 27%.

818
00:40:04,000 --> 00:40:06,160
I'll take one last
question before I

819
00:40:06,160 --> 00:40:09,220
jump to the next
section, if there's any.

820
00:40:09,220 --> 00:40:13,000
Some over Zoom,
as well as online.

821
00:40:13,000 --> 00:40:15,550
I'll ask some.

822
00:40:15,550 --> 00:40:17,253
Let me see.

823
00:40:17,253 --> 00:40:19,420
Someone's wondering is there
still any point of GANs

824
00:40:19,420 --> 00:40:21,460
for image and video
generation, or have they

825
00:40:21,460 --> 00:40:24,920
been fully replaced basically
with diffusion models?

826
00:40:24,920 --> 00:40:28,700
I won't say they have been fully
replaced, because as we saw,

827
00:40:28,700 --> 00:40:31,430
diffusion models are still
sequential in nature.

828
00:40:31,430 --> 00:40:35,000
So if you are looking for really
ultra real-time generation,

829
00:40:35,000 --> 00:40:36,550
I think GANs are
still the way to go,

830
00:40:36,550 --> 00:40:39,140
and many companies are
in fact using them.

831
00:40:39,140 --> 00:40:42,090
So if you're looking for really
cool one-shot generation,

832
00:40:42,090 --> 00:40:44,360
I think GANs is the way.

833
00:40:44,360 --> 00:40:47,390
So there's this literature
around time step distillation

834
00:40:47,390 --> 00:40:50,930
that basically looks at reducing
the number of time steps

835
00:40:50,930 --> 00:40:53,630
or inference steps that you
need in order to produce

836
00:40:53,630 --> 00:40:54,690
a good quality image.

837
00:40:54,690 --> 00:40:56,930
There you need a
GAN loss actually.

838
00:40:56,930 --> 00:41:00,210
So GANs are not going to
be completely replaced yet.

839
00:41:00,210 --> 00:41:03,750
Because I thought GANs had a lot
of issues like mode collapse--

840
00:41:03,750 --> 00:41:05,430
Oh yeah, they have.

841
00:41:05,430 --> 00:41:07,730
But if you have got
specific use cases

842
00:41:07,730 --> 00:41:09,840
and if you have got a
fairly good data set,

843
00:41:09,840 --> 00:41:11,485
you can still train a good GAN.

844
00:41:11,485 --> 00:41:14,810

845
00:41:14,810 --> 00:41:19,640
I can ask one more
this time from online.

846
00:41:19,640 --> 00:41:22,340
Image generation generally
requires a lot of data

847
00:41:22,340 --> 00:41:24,600
to train, especially
for diffusion models.

848
00:41:24,600 --> 00:41:26,960
Are there techniques or
architectural choices

849
00:41:26,960 --> 00:41:30,590
for low data regimes?

850
00:41:30,590 --> 00:41:31,970
That's a good one.

851
00:41:31,970 --> 00:41:32,990
Short answer is no.

852
00:41:32,990 --> 00:41:36,360

853
00:41:36,360 --> 00:41:41,290
I think it's kind of correlated
with your use case a bit.

854
00:41:41,290 --> 00:41:42,970
For medical imaging,
probably you

855
00:41:42,970 --> 00:41:45,410
do not need a whole
lot of diversity.

856
00:41:45,410 --> 00:41:51,140
But if you need a fairly well
and generic generative model,

857
00:41:51,140 --> 00:41:53,110
I think you need to
train it on a lot of data

858
00:41:53,110 --> 00:41:55,610
to inherit all the biases
that you are looking for.

859
00:41:55,610 --> 00:41:59,080
If you want to train on the
distribution of natural images

860
00:41:59,080 --> 00:42:03,590
and you want the model to
always produce realistic images,

861
00:42:03,590 --> 00:42:06,710
I think you need to
have a lot of data--

862
00:42:06,710 --> 00:42:08,220
at least a diverse data.

863
00:42:08,220 --> 00:42:11,440

864
00:42:11,440 --> 00:42:14,600
Because nowadays for
LLMs, there's zero-shot,

865
00:42:14,600 --> 00:42:16,840
few-shot learning
after you pre-trained--

866
00:42:16,840 --> 00:42:19,090
I'm going to talk a bit
about in-context learning

867
00:42:19,090 --> 00:42:20,150
for diffusion models.

868
00:42:20,150 --> 00:42:26,210
But you are assuming you have
access to a pre-trained model.

869
00:42:26,210 --> 00:42:28,960

870
00:42:28,960 --> 00:42:30,670
So there you go.

871
00:42:30,670 --> 00:42:32,470
All right.

872
00:42:32,470 --> 00:42:37,930
I'm going to start
this leg of the talk

873
00:42:37,930 --> 00:42:42,790
by discussing the quadratic
time and memory complexity

874
00:42:42,790 --> 00:42:47,410
argument a bit., because now
we are in the image generation

875
00:42:47,410 --> 00:42:47,990
territory.

876
00:42:47,990 --> 00:42:52,390
And when you are trying to
generate really high resolution

877
00:42:52,390 --> 00:42:57,190
images, like 4K images, vanilla
attention becomes extremely

878
00:42:57,190 --> 00:43:01,210
prohibitive, even if you were
to operate on the latent space.

879
00:43:01,210 --> 00:43:04,180
Take a look at the
dimensions of the latents.

880
00:43:04,180 --> 00:43:08,710
Let's say your number of
latent channels is 16,

881
00:43:08,710 --> 00:43:12,650
and your latent
dimensions are 512, 512.

882
00:43:12,650 --> 00:43:15,590
It's still way too large--

883
00:43:15,590 --> 00:43:19,640
way larger than what we are
used to seeing in the VLM space.

884
00:43:19,640 --> 00:43:22,850
And I have some dummy
computations here.

885
00:43:22,850 --> 00:43:25,100
This is, of course, not
using flash attention.

886
00:43:25,100 --> 00:43:28,940
If I were to give you
some dummy estimates,

887
00:43:28,940 --> 00:43:34,370
it would be like 190 GBs
in floating-point 16.

888
00:43:34,370 --> 00:43:38,000
And this is all like
reasonable defaults.

889
00:43:38,000 --> 00:43:40,910
I have 24 attention heads.

890
00:43:40,910 --> 00:43:42,910
I've got batch size one.

891
00:43:42,910 --> 00:43:45,730
The sequence length
is flattened out.

892
00:43:45,730 --> 00:43:48,430
I would need 190 GB.

893
00:43:48,430 --> 00:43:50,560
And this is, of course,
not using flash attention.

894
00:43:50,560 --> 00:43:55,420
But you get an idea of the
prohibitive computation space

895
00:43:55,420 --> 00:43:58,330
of the quadratic
attention that we usually

896
00:43:58,330 --> 00:44:00,220
use in diffusion
models if we were

897
00:44:00,220 --> 00:44:06,070
to deal with really high quality
and high resolution images.

898
00:44:06,070 --> 00:44:07,730
So what could we do?

899
00:44:07,730 --> 00:44:09,910
Two simple things
that we could do

900
00:44:09,910 --> 00:44:12,760
is operate on an even
more compressed space,

901
00:44:12,760 --> 00:44:16,850
like this-- this
1, 16, 512, 512.

902
00:44:16,850 --> 00:44:18,980
This space is already
compressed enough.

903
00:44:18,980 --> 00:44:22,550
But could we even increase
the compression ratio further?

904
00:44:22,550 --> 00:44:26,200
And then the second
obvious thing

905
00:44:26,200 --> 00:44:28,540
could be to use some
form of linear attention

906
00:44:28,540 --> 00:44:32,170
that doesn't do the n
cross in multiplication.

907
00:44:32,170 --> 00:44:36,590
So that brings me to
my next architecture,

908
00:44:36,590 --> 00:44:38,150
which is the SANA architecture.

909
00:44:38,150 --> 00:44:39,860
And it uses both.

910
00:44:39,860 --> 00:44:43,730
It uses an even more
compressed latent space,

911
00:44:43,730 --> 00:44:46,720
and it also uses
a linear variant

912
00:44:46,720 --> 00:44:49,690
of the attention mechanism.

913
00:44:49,690 --> 00:44:51,970
So let's see.

914
00:44:51,970 --> 00:44:55,670
So our linear attention
is on the other hand.

915
00:44:55,670 --> 00:44:58,840
And of course, you might
expect some performance loss

916
00:44:58,840 --> 00:45:03,350
with the linear complexity
of the attention mechanism.

917
00:45:03,350 --> 00:45:05,200
And to compensate
for the performance

918
00:45:05,200 --> 00:45:07,550
lost, we use Mix-FFN.

919
00:45:07,550 --> 00:45:10,190
And I'm going to get into
the details in a bit.

920
00:45:10,190 --> 00:45:12,850
But just wanted to give
you a quick overview.

921
00:45:12,850 --> 00:45:17,170
So SANA does self-attention--
self linear attention,

922
00:45:17,170 --> 00:45:17,840
as you will.

923
00:45:17,840 --> 00:45:19,900
But it still does
cross attention

924
00:45:19,900 --> 00:45:23,080
to model the dependencies
between the noisy latents,

925
00:45:23,080 --> 00:45:27,080
as well as the text prompts
that you are providing it too.

926
00:45:27,080 --> 00:45:29,890
And then there's no
n square computation

927
00:45:29,890 --> 00:45:32,860
happening in the
self-attention, and the equation

928
00:45:32,860 --> 00:45:33,740
makes that clear.

929
00:45:33,740 --> 00:45:36,430
So we have got shared
terms, shared terms computed

930
00:45:36,430 --> 00:45:38,090
from the kV projections.

931
00:45:38,090 --> 00:45:40,760
And these are reused
for all the queries.

932
00:45:40,760 --> 00:45:45,160
And this way, we are
basically and effectively not

933
00:45:45,160 --> 00:45:47,090
doing the n cross
in multiplications.

934
00:45:47,090 --> 00:45:50,890
And all of the multiplications
are upper bound to n.

935
00:45:50,890 --> 00:45:53,020
So that's why we are
able to reduce from order

936
00:45:53,020 --> 00:45:55,120
of mn squared to order of n.

937
00:45:55,120 --> 00:45:58,390
And then, as I was
mentioning, there

938
00:45:58,390 --> 00:46:00,970
has to be some form
of accountability

939
00:46:00,970 --> 00:46:04,790
as we are not using the
quadratic attention mechanism.

940
00:46:04,790 --> 00:46:07,460
So we use Mix-FFN blocks.

941
00:46:07,460 --> 00:46:10,000
It's basically a bunch of
inverted residual blocks

942
00:46:10,000 --> 00:46:12,310
and pointwise
convolutions in order

943
00:46:12,310 --> 00:46:14,150
to model the local dependencies.

944
00:46:14,150 --> 00:46:15,610
Because it turns
out that when you

945
00:46:15,610 --> 00:46:18,230
take the softmax
out of the picture,

946
00:46:18,230 --> 00:46:20,870
you lose all the
notion of locality.

947
00:46:20,870 --> 00:46:22,300
Let's not say all--

948
00:46:22,300 --> 00:46:26,720
majority of the locality
notions are taken out also.

949
00:46:26,720 --> 00:46:30,830
So that's why you need some
components to also model

950
00:46:30,830 --> 00:46:32,430
the locality aspect.

951
00:46:32,430 --> 00:46:35,690
And Mix-FFN blocks
are used for that.

952
00:46:35,690 --> 00:46:39,950
And for the first time SANA got
rid of the positional embedding

953
00:46:39,950 --> 00:46:40,530
bit.

954
00:46:40,530 --> 00:46:44,910
So it's very funny to say it--

955
00:46:44,910 --> 00:46:45,410
NoPe.

956
00:46:45,410 --> 00:46:47,300
So no positional embeddings.

957
00:46:47,300 --> 00:46:50,510
And mixed blocks actually
helps because you

958
00:46:50,510 --> 00:46:52,830
have a bunch of convolutional
blocks in there.

959
00:46:52,830 --> 00:46:56,250
So we are still returning to
the convolutional argument,

960
00:46:56,250 --> 00:46:57,320
but not so much.

961
00:46:57,320 --> 00:46:59,520
It's not fully convolutional.

962
00:46:59,520 --> 00:47:01,640
You just have a few
convolutional layers

963
00:47:01,640 --> 00:47:02,670
thrown in there.

964
00:47:02,670 --> 00:47:05,100
So it's largely DiT-based.

965
00:47:05,100 --> 00:47:07,590
But instead of
using a linear MLP,

966
00:47:07,590 --> 00:47:11,060
you have two
convolutional layers

967
00:47:11,060 --> 00:47:14,820
to account for the
local interactions.

968
00:47:14,820 --> 00:47:17,600

969
00:47:17,600 --> 00:47:19,550
And it performs really well.

970
00:47:19,550 --> 00:47:22,550
For its compact size,
it performs fairly well.

971
00:47:22,550 --> 00:47:26,750
I must say that we shouldn't
take into account the kernel

972
00:47:26,750 --> 00:47:31,470
fusion and the fancy DPM
solver that they're using.

973
00:47:31,470 --> 00:47:35,150
But up until the fourth
row, I think it's still

974
00:47:35,150 --> 00:47:36,450
performing fairly well.

975
00:47:36,450 --> 00:47:40,080
It's giving very decent
general performance.

976
00:47:40,080 --> 00:47:43,650
It's giving very
decent DPG performance.

977
00:47:43,650 --> 00:47:49,250
And these are all metrics that
assess a given image in terms

978
00:47:49,250 --> 00:47:52,820
of compositionality, in
terms of their fidelity,

979
00:47:52,820 --> 00:47:56,340
in terms of their overall
quality, and so on.

980
00:47:56,340 --> 00:47:59,810
And these metrics are fairly
well-grounded in terms

981
00:47:59,810 --> 00:48:00,415
of reality.

982
00:48:00,415 --> 00:48:05,270

983
00:48:05,270 --> 00:48:11,180
And this is probably going to
be the final flavor of attention

984
00:48:11,180 --> 00:48:15,150
that I'm going to discuss before
I move on to other topics.

985
00:48:15,150 --> 00:48:18,350
And this is, I think, a bit new.

986
00:48:18,350 --> 00:48:21,830
And also new in the sense
that no one in the VLM space

987
00:48:21,830 --> 00:48:23,130
really does it.

988
00:48:23,130 --> 00:48:27,290
So what happens if
we were to model

989
00:48:27,290 --> 00:48:30,900
dependencies of the different
modalities in separate spaces?

990
00:48:30,900 --> 00:48:32,990
So let's see.

991
00:48:32,990 --> 00:48:38,340
And one motivation behind
this could be text embeddings.

992
00:48:38,340 --> 00:48:41,870
Let's say you are computing text
embeddings with a large language

993
00:48:41,870 --> 00:48:42,600
model.

994
00:48:42,600 --> 00:48:45,920
You will end up inheriting
a lot of bias from it.

995
00:48:45,920 --> 00:48:49,500
The unidirectionality
bias will be there.

996
00:48:49,500 --> 00:48:52,310
If you are using a standard
autoregressive large language

997
00:48:52,310 --> 00:48:54,560
model, you will
end up inheriting

998
00:48:54,560 --> 00:48:56,780
the unidirectionality bias.

999
00:48:56,780 --> 00:49:00,380
So you will have
bias, and they might

1000
00:49:00,380 --> 00:49:07,740
creep into your generative model
in all sorts of different ways.

1001
00:49:07,740 --> 00:49:13,250
So one idea could be to do QKV
projections but separately.

1002
00:49:13,250 --> 00:49:16,260
You do QKV projections
on the text embeddings,

1003
00:49:16,260 --> 00:49:21,380
and you also maintain another
set of QKV projection parameters

1004
00:49:21,380 --> 00:49:23,290
for your noisy latents.

1005
00:49:23,290 --> 00:49:25,830
And then you concatenate
them before you

1006
00:49:25,830 --> 00:49:26,890
compute the attention.

1007
00:49:26,890 --> 00:49:30,630
So you operate on a
concatenated representation

1008
00:49:30,630 --> 00:49:33,450
before you actually
do the attention.

1009
00:49:33,450 --> 00:49:37,410
And conceptually, this
becomes basically this.

1010
00:49:37,410 --> 00:49:41,910
So you project your QKV
stuff on your image latents

1011
00:49:41,910 --> 00:49:44,890
separately from the
text embeddings.

1012
00:49:44,890 --> 00:49:48,930
You concatenate them before
you actually compute attention.

1013
00:49:48,930 --> 00:49:51,430
So this is basically it.

1014
00:49:51,430 --> 00:49:54,150
So this is MMDiT that was
introduced in the Stable

1015
00:49:54,150 --> 00:49:56,040
Diffusion 3 paper.

1016
00:49:56,040 --> 00:49:58,440
And their motivation
was to get rid

1017
00:49:58,440 --> 00:50:01,740
of the different biases
that might be there

1018
00:50:01,740 --> 00:50:02,860
in the text embeddings.

1019
00:50:02,860 --> 00:50:06,150
And they also showed how
qualitatively different text

1020
00:50:06,150 --> 00:50:08,860
embeddings can be from
the image embeddings.

1021
00:50:08,860 --> 00:50:11,070
So I guess that
kind of gives you

1022
00:50:11,070 --> 00:50:15,420
a hint about why we might need
different text embeddings,

1023
00:50:15,420 --> 00:50:18,000
to not end up inheriting
each other's biases

1024
00:50:18,000 --> 00:50:22,130
and have more
diversity in the mix.

1025
00:50:22,130 --> 00:50:24,960
So it basically looks like this.

1026
00:50:24,960 --> 00:50:27,330
So it might feel a
little complicated.

1027
00:50:27,330 --> 00:50:29,840
But the reason
why it's so big is

1028
00:50:29,840 --> 00:50:32,120
because we have
separate projection

1029
00:50:32,120 --> 00:50:35,310
matrices for the different
modalities that we are modeling.

1030
00:50:35,310 --> 00:50:40,620
So on the left-hand side,
we have got the captions.

1031
00:50:40,620 --> 00:50:43,230
And on the right-hand side,
we have got the noisy latents.

1032
00:50:43,230 --> 00:50:45,950
And we have got
separate QKV projections

1033
00:50:45,950 --> 00:50:49,950
and adaptive layer-norm matrices
for the separate modalities.

1034
00:50:49,950 --> 00:50:51,870
So that's why it feels big.

1035
00:50:51,870 --> 00:50:54,625
But conceptually
it's basically this.

1036
00:50:54,625 --> 00:50:58,790

1037
00:50:58,790 --> 00:51:01,520
So you have got separate
adaptive layer-norm matrices

1038
00:51:01,520 --> 00:51:02,910
for the separate modalities.

1039
00:51:02,910 --> 00:51:05,660
You have got separate
QKV projection matrices.

1040
00:51:05,660 --> 00:51:09,080
You have got separate
output projection matrices.

1041
00:51:09,080 --> 00:51:11,900
And then you have got
separate-- everything separate.

1042
00:51:11,900 --> 00:51:15,500
Everything is separate
from the two modalities

1043
00:51:15,500 --> 00:51:16,975
that we are interested in.

1044
00:51:16,975 --> 00:51:20,120

1045
00:51:20,120 --> 00:51:26,300
So in a way, it gives a way
to coevolve the two embeddings

1046
00:51:26,300 --> 00:51:28,100
from the two
different modalities

1047
00:51:28,100 --> 00:51:30,620
that we are working with
for the given task, which is

1048
00:51:30,620 --> 00:51:32,540
image generation in this case.

1049
00:51:32,540 --> 00:51:37,380
And also, if we are very
used to cross-attention,

1050
00:51:37,380 --> 00:51:40,670
you might want to ask if we
were to compute attention

1051
00:51:40,670 --> 00:51:43,500
in this way, how do we do
masking in the first place?

1052
00:51:43,500 --> 00:51:45,540
And that's actually an
active area of research.

1053
00:51:45,540 --> 00:51:49,050
We do not how to do
masks holistically,

1054
00:51:49,050 --> 00:51:54,200
if we were to do a MMDiT
variant of attention.

1055
00:51:54,200 --> 00:51:58,340
And then, modulation
happens with both time steps

1056
00:51:58,340 --> 00:52:02,210
and the conditional embedding
that you are operating with.

1057
00:52:02,210 --> 00:52:07,010
So Stable Diffusion 3 uses a
different set of pooled text

1058
00:52:07,010 --> 00:52:10,670
embeddings that are usually
computed from CLIP, and not

1059
00:52:10,670 --> 00:52:12,240
the T5-based text encoder.

1060
00:52:12,240 --> 00:52:15,630
So again, you need
some form of diversity

1061
00:52:15,630 --> 00:52:19,080
to not end up inheriting the
bias from the other text encoder

1062
00:52:19,080 --> 00:52:21,630
that you had.

1063
00:52:21,630 --> 00:52:25,600
And Stable Diffusion
3 uses three,

1064
00:52:25,600 --> 00:52:28,120
but they can be mixed and
matched during inference.

1065
00:52:28,120 --> 00:52:32,070
They showed that you
need the T5 if you

1066
00:52:32,070 --> 00:52:34,630
need to have really good
text rendering capabilities.

1067
00:52:34,630 --> 00:52:38,700
But other than
that, you can still

1068
00:52:38,700 --> 00:52:41,380
do a lot with the two
clips that they use.

1069
00:52:41,380 --> 00:52:44,190
And you can drop the T5 if
you are not solely focused

1070
00:52:44,190 --> 00:52:46,175
on text rendering tasks.

1071
00:52:46,175 --> 00:52:50,400

1072
00:52:50,400 --> 00:52:53,050
And of course, it
matters quite a bit.

1073
00:52:53,050 --> 00:52:57,040
You might want to ask,
does MMDiT matter at all?

1074
00:52:57,040 --> 00:52:59,320
And it turns out
that it does matter.

1075
00:52:59,320 --> 00:53:03,610
They tried all forms of
different attention variants.

1076
00:53:03,610 --> 00:53:06,190
They tried crossDiT,
they tried UViT,

1077
00:53:06,190 --> 00:53:09,090
and MMDiT seems to be the
variant that gives you

1078
00:53:09,090 --> 00:53:10,750
the lowest validation loss.

1079
00:53:10,750 --> 00:53:14,250
And they also show that
validation loss is fairly well

1080
00:53:14,250 --> 00:53:17,010
correlated with the
kind of image generation

1081
00:53:17,010 --> 00:53:22,050
metrics that we care about,
such as [? Geneva ?] and so on.

1082
00:53:22,050 --> 00:53:25,720
And it scales fairly well,
but it needs QK-normalization.

1083
00:53:25,720 --> 00:53:28,180
And thanks to the
concurrent set of works,

1084
00:53:28,180 --> 00:53:32,230
they didn't have to reinvent
QK-norm from the scratch.

1085
00:53:32,230 --> 00:53:37,870
So thanks to developments in the
regular transformer literature,

1086
00:53:37,870 --> 00:53:41,490
they were able to just use
QK-norm to solve the training

1087
00:53:41,490 --> 00:53:44,400
instability issues.

1088
00:53:44,400 --> 00:53:46,560
And I think Stable
Diffusion 3 is

1089
00:53:46,560 --> 00:53:49,080
basically incomplete
without this picture.

1090
00:53:49,080 --> 00:53:53,070
And I'm not showing this
picture just for fun,

1091
00:53:53,070 --> 00:53:57,930
but also because it shows
how complex is this prompt,

1092
00:53:57,930 --> 00:54:00,600
and how well Stable
Diffusion 3 was

1093
00:54:00,600 --> 00:54:05,920
able to interpret this prompt
and get us a creature like this.

1094
00:54:05,920 --> 00:54:09,150
So I must give credits to the
authors of Stable Diffusion

1095
00:54:09,150 --> 00:54:12,130
3 who came up with
these kinds of prompts,

1096
00:54:12,130 --> 00:54:13,820
but it does fairly well.

1097
00:54:13,820 --> 00:54:18,130
So it was among the
first kinds of models

1098
00:54:18,130 --> 00:54:21,530
that shows impressive
prompt following ability,

1099
00:54:21,530 --> 00:54:26,960
and also while also preserving
the details that we care about.

1100
00:54:26,960 --> 00:54:30,760
So that was cool.

1101
00:54:30,760 --> 00:54:34,130
And MMDiT didn't stop here.

1102
00:54:34,130 --> 00:54:38,800
There are different flavors of
MMDiT that I wanted to discuss.

1103
00:54:38,800 --> 00:54:43,300
In Stable Diffusion 3,
all the transformer blocks

1104
00:54:43,300 --> 00:54:47,240
followed the MMDiT
flavor of attention,

1105
00:54:47,240 --> 00:54:49,820
and it's computationally
demanding.

1106
00:54:49,820 --> 00:54:52,790
If you have worked with
transformer encoders,

1107
00:54:52,790 --> 00:54:55,390
you would appreciate
the computation

1108
00:54:55,390 --> 00:54:59,380
and the computational
intensity of MMDiTs,

1109
00:54:59,380 --> 00:55:03,700
because you are having to
maintain separate projection

1110
00:55:03,700 --> 00:55:05,710
matrices for the
different modalities

1111
00:55:05,710 --> 00:55:06,950
that you are working with.

1112
00:55:06,950 --> 00:55:09,690
So it's computationally
extremely demanding.

1113
00:55:09,690 --> 00:55:14,880
So maybe we could combine MMDiT
blocks and regular DiT blocks

1114
00:55:14,880 --> 00:55:18,510
to be able to better
utilize the flops.

1115
00:55:18,510 --> 00:55:21,310
So conceptually
this becomes this.

1116
00:55:21,310 --> 00:55:25,450
So you have some number
of memory blocks.

1117
00:55:25,450 --> 00:55:27,930
You concatenate the
final representation

1118
00:55:27,930 --> 00:55:31,230
and then you operate on
the concatenate space

1119
00:55:31,230 --> 00:55:33,670
and basically do vanilla it.

1120
00:55:33,670 --> 00:55:36,390
You do not do MMDiT
and you end up

1121
00:55:36,390 --> 00:55:38,880
utilizing the flops
a little better.

1122
00:55:38,880 --> 00:55:42,330
And that's exactly what recent
models like Flux from Black

1123
00:55:42,330 --> 00:55:44,340
Forest labs, they do.

1124
00:55:44,340 --> 00:55:51,630
So another twisty one could
be you have modality A,

1125
00:55:51,630 --> 00:55:54,180
you compute output
A by passing it

1126
00:55:54,180 --> 00:55:56,380
through a bunch of
transformer blocks.

1127
00:55:56,380 --> 00:55:59,620
And then you have got modality
B, let's say text embeddings.

1128
00:55:59,620 --> 00:56:02,350
You pass it through another
set of transformer blocks.

1129
00:56:02,350 --> 00:56:06,420
You concatenate these
outputs, and then you

1130
00:56:06,420 --> 00:56:09,280
pass them through another
set of transformer blocks.

1131
00:56:09,280 --> 00:56:12,870
And then you basically
have your final output.

1132
00:56:12,870 --> 00:56:15,480
And in this way,
you can configure

1133
00:56:15,480 --> 00:56:20,400
all the different blocks
in their own manner.

1134
00:56:20,400 --> 00:56:23,970
The transformer
blocks for modality A

1135
00:56:23,970 --> 00:56:26,370
could be different from
the transformer blocks

1136
00:56:26,370 --> 00:56:29,280
that you would use for
modality B. So this way

1137
00:56:29,280 --> 00:56:32,760
you have a greater level
of flexibility and control.

1138
00:56:32,760 --> 00:56:36,130
And that's what the
Lumina2 work did.

1139
00:56:36,130 --> 00:56:39,310
And as we can see,
for conditions,

1140
00:56:39,310 --> 00:56:42,270
they pass it through a separate
set of transformer blocks.

1141
00:56:42,270 --> 00:56:45,390
For noisy latents, they pass
it through a separate set

1142
00:56:45,390 --> 00:56:46,500
of transformer blocks.

1143
00:56:46,500 --> 00:56:49,120
And they end up
concatenating them,

1144
00:56:49,120 --> 00:56:52,510
and then again they have another
set of transformer blocks.

1145
00:56:52,510 --> 00:56:58,560
So transformer blocks on the
left-hand side in the RHS

1146
00:56:58,560 --> 00:57:02,400
big block can be different from
the transformer blocks used

1147
00:57:02,400 --> 00:57:04,990
to model the noisy latents.

1148
00:57:04,990 --> 00:57:08,840
So you have got greater
flexibility and control.

1149
00:57:08,840 --> 00:57:12,270

1150
00:57:12,270 --> 00:57:15,330
And I want to give
you a sense of how

1151
00:57:15,330 --> 00:57:18,450
we can think of simplifying
all of this design,

1152
00:57:18,450 --> 00:57:20,430
because it might
feel complicated

1153
00:57:20,430 --> 00:57:21,820
at this point in time.

1154
00:57:21,820 --> 00:57:27,010
But I don't know, maybe we
can simplify it quite a bit.

1155
00:57:27,010 --> 00:57:29,430
But I want to quickly
