training, I would say.

1255
01:01:58,420 --> 01:02:01,570
You have more stable
representations

1256
01:02:01,570 --> 01:02:05,180
across the blocks.

1257
01:02:05,180 --> 01:02:07,245
But for images, there
are certain kind

1258
01:02:07,245 --> 01:02:08,620
of characteristics
that you would

1259
01:02:08,620 --> 01:02:11,440
want to model beyond the
standard representations

1260
01:02:11,440 --> 01:02:13,580
that you are computing.

1261
01:02:13,580 --> 01:02:18,790
And you will have to let
those features flow freely

1262
01:02:18,790 --> 01:02:20,330
into your transformer blocks.

1263
01:02:20,330 --> 01:02:22,610
And that's why you need
the modulation parameters.

1264
01:02:22,610 --> 01:02:26,140
Otherwise the interaction
in between the normalization

1265
01:02:26,140 --> 01:02:29,080
parameters and your
regular attention

1266
01:02:29,080 --> 01:02:32,150
features or whatever MLP
features that you are computing,

1267
01:02:32,150 --> 01:02:36,215
they don't get to interact
in a way that will benefit

1268
01:02:36,215 --> 01:02:37,340
the generation performance.

1269
01:02:37,340 --> 01:02:40,870
If you were to deal with just
understanding or maybe just

1270
01:02:40,870 --> 01:02:42,640
discriminative
performance, it wouldn't

1271
01:02:42,640 --> 01:02:44,450
have mattered that much.

1272
01:02:44,450 --> 01:02:47,450
But if you care about
stylistic aspects and fidelity,

1273
01:02:47,450 --> 01:02:51,490
you need to modulate the
additional features that you are

1274
01:02:51,490 --> 01:02:55,030
getting from the visual cues.

1275
01:02:55,030 --> 01:02:57,430
Yeah.

1276
01:02:57,430 --> 01:03:00,050
Any other questions?

1277
01:03:00,050 --> 01:03:00,550
Yeah.

1278
01:03:00,550 --> 01:03:01,420
It's like a mission.

1279
01:03:01,420 --> 01:03:03,212
We don't know yet how
[INAUDIBLE] masking--

1280
01:03:03,212 --> 01:03:04,270

1281
01:03:04,270 --> 01:03:05,990
For MMDiT.

1282
01:03:05,990 --> 01:03:07,110
Yeah.

1283
01:03:07,110 --> 01:03:09,350
[INAUDIBLE] What's
the next best thing?

1284
01:03:09,350 --> 01:03:11,210
Is there an
alternative to masking?

1285
01:03:11,210 --> 01:03:12,265
Maybe that's a hunch.

1286
01:03:12,265 --> 01:03:14,920

1287
01:03:14,920 --> 01:03:16,820
Why would you need masking.

1288
01:03:16,820 --> 01:03:20,770
That's a good question
to ask for MMDiT.

1289
01:03:20,770 --> 01:03:24,820
So MMDiT was done in
order to benefit--

1290
01:03:24,820 --> 01:03:29,110
in order to get away with
the unidirectionality bias

1291
01:03:29,110 --> 01:03:32,290
that you may have in your text
embedding representations.

1292
01:03:32,290 --> 01:03:33,640
So that was one.

1293
01:03:33,640 --> 01:03:35,960
Second, if you have
really long prompts,

1294
01:03:35,960 --> 01:03:39,130
you do not need to compute
masks in the first place.

1295
01:03:39,130 --> 01:03:42,160
And it also turns out
that it's always better

1296
01:03:42,160 --> 01:03:44,530
to have long prompts,
like descriptive prompts,

1297
01:03:44,530 --> 01:03:47,330
rather than having to
have short prompts.

1298
01:03:47,330 --> 01:03:51,020
So that is kind of a hand-wavy
way to answer this question.

1299
01:03:51,020 --> 01:03:55,390
But long story cut short, it's
really non-trivial to add masks

1300
01:03:55,390 --> 01:03:57,530
when you are doing
MMDiT attention.

1301
01:03:57,530 --> 01:03:59,440
So there you go.

1302
01:03:59,440 --> 01:04:03,490
[OVERLAPPING VOICES] was about
doing separately QKV on both

1303
01:04:03,490 --> 01:04:04,030
text--

1304
01:04:04,030 --> 01:04:05,290
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1305
01:04:05,290 --> 01:04:08,150
How about on the image side?

1306
01:04:08,150 --> 01:04:10,720
So when you are doing
the image interaction

1307
01:04:10,720 --> 01:04:13,100
you could in theory do masks.

1308
01:04:13,100 --> 01:04:16,270
But again, how would
you frame the problem?

1309
01:04:16,270 --> 01:04:18,590
Where would you end
up adding the masks?

1310
01:04:18,590 --> 01:04:21,420
If you are just doing
text to image, maybe.

1311
01:04:21,420 --> 01:04:25,670
Maybe if you have another
form of conditional control,

1312
01:04:25,670 --> 01:04:28,760
maybe you would want to mask
some of the interactions there.

1313
01:04:28,760 --> 01:04:31,160
But if you are just
restricted to text-to-image,

1314
01:04:31,160 --> 01:04:34,390
where would you add the
masks in the first place?

1315
01:04:34,390 --> 01:04:36,170
What I'm asking is
to avoid masking.

1316
01:04:36,170 --> 01:04:36,670
Oh.

1317
01:04:36,670 --> 01:04:38,027
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1318
01:04:38,027 --> 01:04:40,610
Probably that's why they didn't
do masking in the first place.

1319
01:04:40,610 --> 01:04:42,550
Maybe their motivations--
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1320
01:04:42,550 --> 01:04:45,370
Exactly, exactly.

1321
01:04:45,370 --> 01:04:49,600
I'll maybe take one final
question if there's any.

1322
01:04:49,600 --> 01:04:51,940
OK, cool.

1323
01:04:51,940 --> 01:04:54,730
So I also want to leave
you with some ideas

1324
01:04:54,730 --> 01:04:57,680
for simplifying
the design a bit.

1325
01:04:57,680 --> 01:05:01,750
If these things felt a little
complicated, because they do not

1326
01:05:01,750 --> 01:05:03,320
have to be complicated at all.

1327
01:05:03,320 --> 01:05:07,960
We can simplify it quite
a bit, and let's see how.

1328
01:05:07,960 --> 01:05:12,230
So how much is parameter
shading is useful?

1329
01:05:12,230 --> 01:05:17,170
Things like adaLN that we saw
and the work on PixArt-alpha

1330
01:05:17,170 --> 01:05:20,690
that reduce the
computation by 27%.

1331
01:05:20,690 --> 01:05:24,580
How much is that kind of
parameter shading is useful?

1332
01:05:24,580 --> 01:05:27,190
And we saw adaLN already.

1333
01:05:27,190 --> 01:05:30,920
Can we also share QKVO
and the MLP, like Albert?

1334
01:05:30,920 --> 01:05:32,920
This is from back in the days--

1335
01:05:32,920 --> 01:05:35,860
Albert where we shared
all the projection

1336
01:05:35,860 --> 01:05:38,450
matrices for a couple of layers.

1337
01:05:38,450 --> 01:05:41,890
We also shared the MLP
matrices and so on.

1338
01:05:41,890 --> 01:05:45,130
And do we really
need self-attention

1339
01:05:45,130 --> 01:05:48,260
and then cross-attention,
or do we really need MMDiT?

1340
01:05:48,260 --> 01:05:50,570
I think this will probably
answer your question,

1341
01:05:50,570 --> 01:05:52,600
so I look forward to that.

1342
01:05:52,600 --> 01:05:54,670
So do we really
need these things?

1343
01:05:54,670 --> 01:05:58,600
Because this, in a sense,
complicates the design space

1344
01:05:58,600 --> 01:05:59,740
quite a bit.

1345
01:05:59,740 --> 01:06:01,900
Can we simplify it?

1346
01:06:01,900 --> 01:06:04,990
So can we basically
do self-attention

1347
01:06:04,990 --> 01:06:07,390
on this concatenated space?

1348
01:06:07,390 --> 01:06:10,250
We do not have to do
self-attention on noisy latents.

1349
01:06:10,250 --> 01:06:12,010
We do not have to
do cross-attention

1350
01:06:12,010 --> 01:06:13,760
on noisy latents and text.

1351
01:06:13,760 --> 01:06:15,350
We do not have to do MMDiT.

1352
01:06:15,350 --> 01:06:19,900
Can we basically concatenate all
the image tokens and the text

1353
01:06:19,900 --> 01:06:22,340
tokens and compute
self-attention on it?

1354
01:06:22,340 --> 01:06:24,880
Can we do it?

1355
01:06:24,880 --> 01:06:27,520
And text encoders,
what's the secret sauce?

1356
01:06:27,520 --> 01:06:29,960
We are using three,
we are using two--

1357
01:06:29,960 --> 01:06:31,360
what helps?

1358
01:06:31,360 --> 01:06:34,340
What helps the cause of
text-to-image generation

1359
01:06:34,340 --> 01:06:36,250
at the end of the day?

1360
01:06:36,250 --> 01:06:38,540
It turns out you can
simplify things a lot.

1361
01:06:38,540 --> 01:06:42,250
So one thing that you can do
is you can parameter share

1362
01:06:42,250 --> 01:06:42,890
quite a bit.

1363
01:06:42,890 --> 01:06:46,730
You can parameter share QKVO if
you are looking for efficiency.

1364
01:06:46,730 --> 01:06:48,400
You can definitely
parameter share

1365
01:06:48,400 --> 01:06:51,980
the adaptive
layer-norm parameters.

1366
01:06:51,980 --> 01:06:53,920
And you can basically
do self-attention

1367
01:06:53,920 --> 01:06:58,660
on a concatenated representation
space of image tokens and text

1368
01:06:58,660 --> 01:06:59,530
tokens.

1369
01:06:59,530 --> 01:07:03,340
So this is, I think, good news,
but not so much because Apple

1370
01:07:03,340 --> 01:07:05,300
didn't open source this work.

1371
01:07:05,300 --> 01:07:07,040
So we'll have to do our own.

1372
01:07:07,040 --> 01:07:11,270
But this is good to that you can
simplify the design quite a bit.

1373
01:07:11,270 --> 01:07:14,140
So the extreme right-hand
side is the simplified design

1374
01:07:14,140 --> 01:07:15,790
that I was talking about.

1375
01:07:15,790 --> 01:07:20,290
So you are basically operating
on a concatenated space.

1376
01:07:20,290 --> 01:07:23,890
You are reducing the adaptive
layer-norm parameters

1377
01:07:23,890 --> 01:07:24,740
quite a bit.

1378
01:07:24,740 --> 01:07:27,940
And then you are parameter
sharing the QKV projections

1379
01:07:27,940 --> 01:07:31,840
as well as the MLP layers.

1380
01:07:31,840 --> 01:07:36,640
And it turns out to be working
well-- fairly well, I must say,

1381
01:07:36,640 --> 01:07:38,170
in practice.

1382
01:07:38,170 --> 01:07:41,900
So the green one is
the simplified design,

1383
01:07:41,900 --> 01:07:45,770
and it always gets the
lowest amount of loss.

1384
01:07:45,770 --> 01:07:49,300
So yeah.

1385
01:07:49,300 --> 01:07:52,570
And as I was mentioning,
other than sharing

1386
01:07:52,570 --> 01:07:54,170
the adaptive
layer-norm parameters,

1387
01:07:54,170 --> 01:07:59,180
you can also share the QKV
parameters as well as the MLP,

1388
01:07:59,180 --> 01:08:02,450
if you can compromise
some of the quality a bit,

1389
01:08:02,450 --> 01:08:05,320
and if you are only
targeting efficiency.

1390
01:08:05,320 --> 01:08:08,050
And for text encoders,
the Apple folks

1391
01:08:08,050 --> 01:08:12,070
found out that bidirectional
CLIP and text only LLM

1392
01:08:12,070 --> 01:08:12,800
is better.

1393
01:08:12,800 --> 01:08:16,550
So instead of using a
combination of CLIP and T5,

1394
01:08:16,550 --> 01:08:19,819
if you use CLIP and a
regular large language model,

1395
01:08:19,819 --> 01:08:23,319
it's better for text
representations.

1396
01:08:23,319 --> 01:08:25,250
So the performance
is pretty good.

1397
01:08:25,250 --> 01:08:28,370
The final row is Apple's work.

1398
01:08:28,370 --> 01:08:29,680
It's called DiT-Air.

1399
01:08:29,680 --> 01:08:32,359
So turns out fairly well.

1400
01:08:32,359 --> 01:08:36,410
All the bold numbers from DiT.

1401
01:08:36,410 --> 01:08:38,200
So it turns out pretty well.

1402
01:08:38,200 --> 01:08:41,240
So long story cut short, you can
simplify the design quite a bit.

1403
01:08:41,240 --> 01:08:43,859
So you do not need
MMDiT as it turns out.

1404
01:08:43,859 --> 01:08:47,029
But we will see, I guess.

1405
01:08:47,029 --> 01:08:50,210
Now, one burning question you
must be having at this point

1406
01:08:50,210 --> 01:08:53,310
in time is, text-to-image
is liberating,

1407
01:08:53,310 --> 01:08:57,450
but how do I inject
more control--

1408
01:08:57,450 --> 01:08:58,920
more sources of control?

1409
01:08:58,920 --> 01:09:03,260
If I were to do more
structural inputs

1410
01:09:03,260 --> 01:09:05,939
to my text-to-image
model, how do I do that?

1411
01:09:05,939 --> 01:09:09,240
Maybe I want the model to
follow a particular pose.

1412
01:09:09,240 --> 01:09:12,770
Maybe I want the model to
follow a particular segmentation

1413
01:09:12,770 --> 01:09:13,680
map and so on.

1414
01:09:13,680 --> 01:09:14,970
And how do I do that?

1415
01:09:14,970 --> 01:09:17,899
And can I combine multiple
structural signals

1416
01:09:17,899 --> 01:09:19,460
at the same time?

1417
01:09:19,460 --> 01:09:27,350
So one could be to learn maybe
an auxiliary network that

1418
01:09:27,350 --> 01:09:30,140
gives you a way to compute
salient representations

1419
01:09:30,140 --> 01:09:32,520
from your structural
image signals.

1420
01:09:32,520 --> 01:09:34,430
And maybe you then
figure a way out

1421
01:09:34,430 --> 01:09:39,920
to inject those structural
signals into your base diffusion

1422
01:09:39,920 --> 01:09:40,529
model.

1423
01:09:40,529 --> 01:09:41,620
So that could be one.

1424
01:09:41,620 --> 01:09:47,740
And ControlNet, these lines
of work, they basically

1425
01:09:47,740 --> 01:09:49,779
follow this philosophy.

1426
01:09:49,779 --> 01:09:53,740
And then maybe you could also
change the base diffusion

1427
01:09:53,740 --> 01:09:54,920
transformer model.

1428
01:09:54,920 --> 01:09:57,940
You basically increase
your input channels

1429
01:09:57,940 --> 01:10:01,690
to accept more controls.

1430
01:10:01,690 --> 01:10:05,620
And that's what the
FluxControl framework does.

1431
01:10:05,620 --> 01:10:08,890
And maybe you could also
learn a small adapter network

1432
01:10:08,890 --> 01:10:12,340
to model the dependencies
between all your conditions

1433
01:10:12,340 --> 01:10:17,740
and your noisy latent tokens.

1434
01:10:17,740 --> 01:10:20,170
And also one important
call out here

1435
01:10:20,170 --> 01:10:23,440
is structural
control will always

1436
01:10:23,440 --> 01:10:26,320
have spatial correspondence,
but other tasks

1437
01:10:26,320 --> 01:10:29,780
like subject-driven
generation or image edits,

1438
01:10:29,780 --> 01:10:32,210
they may not have direct
spatial correspondence.

1439
01:10:32,210 --> 01:10:34,550
So what do we do in those cases?

1440
01:10:34,550 --> 01:10:40,040
And this is, again, an
active area of research.

1441
01:10:40,040 --> 01:10:44,150
And for videos, well,
RoPE works fairly well

1442
01:10:44,150 --> 01:10:45,530
for positional encoding.

1443
01:10:45,530 --> 01:10:49,160
And as I was mentioning, the
attention computation space

1444
01:10:49,160 --> 01:10:52,620
becomes way more prohibitive
because it's full 3D.

1445
01:10:52,620 --> 01:10:54,800
And if you were
thinking about some form

1446
01:10:54,800 --> 01:10:56,820
of factorized attention,
it doesn't work.

1447
01:10:56,820 --> 01:11:00,080
I have been very
explicit about it.

1448
01:11:00,080 --> 01:11:02,690
And for efficiency, if
you are particularly

1449
01:11:02,690 --> 01:11:07,230
interested in efficiency-related
literature around video models,

1450
01:11:07,230 --> 01:11:10,310
I highly suggest checking
out the LTX-Video work.

1451
01:11:10,310 --> 01:11:12,660
And for performance,
like for fidelity,

1452
01:11:12,660 --> 01:11:17,130
photorealism, and so on, I
think Wan has to be my favorite.

1453
01:11:17,130 --> 01:11:19,010
It's pretty good.

1454
01:11:19,010 --> 01:11:22,580
I have a little demo here.

1455
01:11:22,580 --> 01:11:24,090
It's not that bad.

1456
01:11:24,090 --> 01:11:28,940
So it's still short form
video, but the realism aspect

1457
01:11:28,940 --> 01:11:30,710
and the fidelity
aspect of these videos,

1458
01:11:30,710 --> 01:11:32,335
I think they have
improved quite a bit.

1459
01:11:32,335 --> 01:11:35,450

1460
01:11:35,450 --> 01:11:37,540
And some next generation
architectures.

1461
01:11:37,540 --> 01:11:40,290
Now I'm coming towards
the end of my talk.

1462
01:11:40,290 --> 01:11:42,270
I wanted to also
give you a flavor

1463
01:11:42,270 --> 01:11:44,440
and a sense of next
generation architectures.

1464
01:11:44,440 --> 01:11:46,860
You probably have
this question, how

1465
01:11:46,860 --> 01:11:50,010
do we enable in-context
learning in diffusion models?

1466
01:11:50,010 --> 01:11:52,230
In language model, it's
very common-- zero-shot

1467
01:11:52,230 --> 01:11:53,140
learning and so on.

1468
01:11:53,140 --> 01:11:57,150
How do we enable in-context
learning in diffusion models?

1469
01:11:57,150 --> 01:11:59,280
Current architectures
are clearly not

1470
01:11:59,280 --> 01:12:02,460
sufficient to enable it
in a reasonable time.

1471
01:12:02,460 --> 01:12:05,410
So the basic idea is
you take an LLM model,

1472
01:12:05,410 --> 01:12:07,590
you add some components
so that it also

1473
01:12:07,590 --> 01:12:09,630
becomes adept at
generating images

1474
01:12:09,630 --> 01:12:11,770
like recent works like Bagel.

1475
01:12:11,770 --> 01:12:13,780
Then there are works
like [INAUDIBLE]--

1476
01:12:13,780 --> 01:12:16,810
they basically follow
this area of work.

1477
01:12:16,810 --> 01:12:19,450
They are not necessarily
diffusion-based,

1478
01:12:19,450 --> 01:12:22,710
but they share a
similar philosophy.

1479
01:12:22,710 --> 01:12:25,180
So you basically start
from a pre-trained LLM,

1480
01:12:25,180 --> 01:12:27,750
you add components to it
so that it also becomes

1481
01:12:27,750 --> 01:12:29,320
adept at generating images.

1482
01:12:29,320 --> 01:12:33,400
You could do autoregression on
discrete tokens, for example.

1483
01:12:33,400 --> 01:12:35,720
And you could also do, at
the same time diffusion,

1484
01:12:35,720 --> 01:12:37,170
on the continuous tokens.

1485
01:12:37,170 --> 01:12:40,880
So that's one line of work
that's called transfusion.

1486
01:12:40,880 --> 01:12:45,075
So as I was mentioning, there's
Playground v3, there's FuseDiT,

1487
01:12:45,075 --> 01:12:45,950
there'ss Transfusion.

1488
01:12:45,950 --> 01:12:47,610
FuseDiT is from our group.

1489
01:12:47,610 --> 01:12:49,970
It's the only open source
work that you will probably

1490
01:12:49,970 --> 01:12:54,780
find that tackles this problem
of in-context learning,

1491
01:12:54,780 --> 01:12:57,980
and how do you explore
these architectures

1492
01:12:57,980 --> 01:13:01,310
in a holistic manner.

1493
01:13:01,310 --> 01:13:04,800
And if you are already
feeling inspired--

1494
01:13:04,800 --> 01:13:08,010
I hope you are feeling inspired
to explore these architectures.

1495
01:13:08,010 --> 01:13:10,640
I highly welcome you to
check out the library

1496
01:13:10,640 --> 01:13:12,330
that I work on at Hugging Face.

1497
01:13:12,330 --> 01:13:13,790
It's called diffusers.

1498
01:13:13,790 --> 01:13:16,790
We have got reasonably
clean implementations

1499
01:13:16,790 --> 01:13:19,730
of all the models that
I discussed today,

1500
01:13:19,730 --> 01:13:23,750
which should probably inspire
you to hack into these things

1501
